CinefexSearchData.register("text/27",{"issue":27,"date":"August 1986","pages":[["1","Cover","number 27• $4.95 Canadian - $7.50 cinerex"],["2","Closing pages",""],["3","Contents","cinefex CINEFEX 27-August 1986. Published quarterly at P.O. Box 20027, Riverside, California 92516. Subscriptions (U.S.): four issues - $15; eight issues - $25; twelve issues - $35. Return postage must accompany all unsolicited manuscripts, photos or other materials. Printed by Valley Printers, Riverside, California; color separations by ViviColour, City of Industry, California. Contents copyright © 1986 by Don Shay. All rights reserved. Reproduction in whole or in part without written permission is prohibited. ...the journal of cinematic illusions Aliens editor & publisher Don Shay associate editor Janine Pourroy contributing editors Adam Eisenberg Jody Duncan Shay editorial assistant Ann Dredla FRONT COVER - The alien queen on a quest for vengeance in Aliens. 4 In the seven years since its release, Ridley Scott's Alien has endured as the quintessential science fiction horror film - a stylish thriller and boxoffice favorite that spawned a rash of forgettable clones but somehow defied legitimate efforts to generate a worthy sequel. A fresh approach was clearly in order, but that approach proved evasive until writer-director James Cameron was afforded the opportunity to develop his own scenario - an action-packed roller coaster ride that succeeded admirably in retaining the essential elements of the original without being fettered by them. Despite a studio analyst's estimate that Cameron's Aliens script would cost $35 million to make, producer Gale Anne Hurd mounted the ambitious sequel in England - bringing it in for a remarkably frugal $18 million. Of crucial importance to the cost curtailment effort was the need to keep the film's extensive special effects from spiraling out of control. Striving for high-quality work with low-level technology, Cameron and Hurd assembled a team of professionals that included conceptual designers Syd Mead and Ron Cobb, production designer Peter Lamont, visual effects supervisors Robert Skotak and Dennis Skotak, postproduction supervisor Brian Johnson, alien effects creator Stan Winston and physical effects supervisor John Richardson. These and other effects artisans discuss in detail their work on the film and the time-pressured campaign to bring Aliens into being. Article by Don Shay. A drop-ship makes a landing approach in Aliens - BACK COVER."],["4","Aliens","ALIENS article by Don Shay"],["5","Aliens",""],["6","Aliens","6 • CINEFEX 27"],["7","Aliens","Fifty-seven years have passed. The commercial star freighter Nostromo, along with most of her seven-man crew, has long since been blasted into cosmic dust. The alien juggernaut that decimated her has likewise been destroyed. Only warrant officer Ripley has survived the onslaught. Sealed within a hypersleep capsule and cast adrift in the starship's shuttlecraft, she has floated aimlessly through the heavens for decades. Now rescue The years have been kind to Ripley. Her cryogenic sleep has forestalled the ravages of time and she emerges from the capsule as young and fit as when she entered it more than half a century before. The world around her, however, haschanged. Most painful for her is the realization that family and friends she left behind are no longer still alive. Compounding this personal tragedy is a court of inquiry seemingly intent upon holding Ripley accountable for the destruction of her ship and the loss of her mates. Her sole defense - a persistent recounting of the horrors on planet LV-426 - falls largely on deaf ears, especially since the planet she remembers with utter dread has been colonized without incident for more than twenty years. When all communications from the colony suddenly cease, however, a military task force isassembled to investigate - and as the only available authority on the planet, Ripley is conscripted to join them. \"All they said was, 'Ripley and soldiers,\" recalled James Cameron of his first meeting with executive producers Walter Hill and David Giler on the possibility of generating a sequel to Ridley Scott's ultimate science fiction horror film, Alien. \"They didn't really give me anything specific - just this idea of getting her together with some military types and having them all go back to the planet.\" Cameron, who had earned his wings as an art director and effects technician at Roger Corman's New World Pictures, had subsequently directed the ultra-lowbudget Piranha Il and since had been spending most of his time as a graphic artist and screenwriter. \"I was writing Rambo at the time and I was getting into the whole Vietnam thing, and it occurred to me that 'grunts in space' was a wonderful concept. There's a whole list of science fiction going back to the Twenties that explores the idea of the military in space, but it hadn't really been done in a film. So I took that idea and all the elements from the first film that I liked and thought would be worth retaining, and from there the story crystallized very quickly - in about two days as a matter of fact. I just sort of sat down Aliens photographs copyright © 1986 by Twentieth Century Fox Film Corporation. All rights reserved. Production unit still photography by Robert Penn. Additional photographs courtesy Stan Winston, Pat McClung, Dennis and Robert Skotak, Brian Johnson, John Richardson, Doug Beswick, Phil Notaro and Michael Burnett. Conceptual illustrations courtesy Syd Mead and Ron Cobb. Special thanks to Polly Apostolof Cross, Linden Wineland, Bill Krohn, Geoff Freeman, Stanley Bielecki and Jody Duncan Shay. and drank a lot of coffee and wrote a treatment.\" Before a finished script could be completed, however, lightning struck for Jim Cameron. A screenplay he had cowritten with fellow Corman alumnus Gale Anne Hurd had at last received a production go-ahead. Under Cameron's confident direction, the film - a moderately-budgeted action picture called The Terminator- would become the surprise boxoffice hit of the late 1984 season. \"I think it's probably safe to say that if The Terminator had come out and I had then been approached with the idea of doing Alien 2, 1 probably would have shied away from it. But it didn't happen that way. I wrote The Terminator first. As a result of writing The Terminator, I was offered an opportunity to write Alien 2 - which I accepted with the tentative proviso that it lead to my directing the film. So I began writing. I created the characters, I created the scenario and I got emotionally involved. I had a large creative investment in what I'd done up to that point. Then I did Terminator. The Aliens script wasn't finished yet, but Walter Hill and David Giler decided - since they liked the pages that I'd done - to wait until I'd finished shooting Terminatorto pick up the traces and continue. At that point, I didn't need to do the sequel to Alien, but I wanted to because I liked what I'd created. Once the imagery is in your head, you can't get rid of it. The only catharsis is to make the movie. Then you never have to deal with it again.\" For Cameron, never having to deal with it again meant accepting a killer deadline that put a rush on nearly every phase of sound i Teamed the production from concept design to final sound mix. Teamed once again with his Terminator producer Gale Anne Hurd - a functional alliance that had broadened into marriage during a brief pause in the Aliens negotiations - Cameron launched himself wholeheartedly into the ambitious project. In fashioning his screenplay, Cameron had considerable room to expand and elaborate on the groundwork established by his predecessor. Conveniently, all but one of the original crew members had been killed off, affording ample opportunity for new characters and relationships. Likewise, the principal setting - the claustrophobic Nostromo - had been blown to bits. Even the hostile uninhabited planet presented little in the way of dramatic restrictions since the passage of more than half a century between films enabled Cameron to transform it into a burgeoning earth colony. About the only inviolable element would be the polymorphous alien Ridley Scott had found lurking in the subconscious mind of H.R. Giger. The seminal face-hugger, the embryonic chestburster and the full-grown alien warrior - all its terrifying forms would be reprised... and then some. \"Part of the attraction of doing the film was that it was a design fest,\" said Cameron, \"an opportunity to do all sorts of wonderful hardware. I like hardware a lot. As a filmmaker, I don't want to be a slave to it, but the hardware has to be up to certain standards; and in this story there was plenty of opportunity for that kind of design.\" For starters, Cameron's hardware requirements included the Sulaco - a giant, heavily-armed military transport to ferry Ripley and her compatriots back to the planet. TechSeven years after the release of Alien, writer-director James Cameron and producer Gale Anne Hurd have returned to the planet Acheron for another close encounter with the horrific creatures first unleashed by the hapless crew of the star freighter Nostromo. Aside from the aliens themselves, virtually none of the design elements from the Ridley Scott original were carried over into Aliens. One exception was the Narcissus - a two-foot-long recreation of the Nostromo's shuttlecraft fashioned by modelmaker Jay Roth. / The giant salvage vessel into which the Narcissus is drawn was represented solely by a tabletop miniature of its underbelly section designed by visual effects supervisor Robert Skotak and built by modelmaker Faisil Karim. / Shouldering a video camera, Jim Cameron works out a sequence in the abandoned power plant used to represent the interior of the atmosphere processing station on Acheron. CINEFEX 27 • 7"],["8","Aliens","Dennis Skotak, the Gateway shot was a multi-element incamera effect that opened on a photographed painting panned over to the orbital which was painted on glass constructed in miniature and reflected in via a beamsplitter. Each section had its own separate starfield. In addition, a sun element was introduced and model ships were rigged to pass through on wires. / Conceptual artist Syd Mead was engaged to design the Sulaco - a giant transports Ripley back to hardened squad of colonial marines. Based on his initial reading of the script, Mead rendering that featured a rotating crew compartment up front, a bristling antenna field behind it and a heavilyarmed aft section housing cargo bays and propulsion As devised by Bob and of the earth and then space station - half of and the other half interstellar warship that Acheron with a battleprepared a preliminary systems. 8 - CINEFEX 27 nologically advanced shipboard interiors were obligatory - hypersleep vault, mess hall and armory - as was a massive cargo hold capable of housing a pair of suborbital shuttlecraft, an armored personnel carrier and a large anthropomorphic forklift. Also required were several settings within the earthcircling Gateway station where Ripley is interrogated following her rescue, plus others on the now-named and colonized planet of Acheron where a cluster of prefab buildings lies nestled in the shadow of an enormous atmosphere processing plant. On a smaller scale, the script also called for a full range of hightech military weaponry. Last, but certainly not least, were the \"My attitude on the alien was to render unto Giger what was Giger's but hopefully not limit myself too much in the process. It was a given that we use most of the organisms already established - with the option possibly of embellishing on what they could do. In the first film, for example, the face-hugger - after its leap onto John Hurt's face - appears simply as an inert form. In Aliens, we changed that. Now it has the physical capability, should it miss on that first leap, to run around on its eight legs and leap again - which made for a really interesting sequence. To a lesser extent, we were able to alter the capabilities of the alien warriors. We knew from the start we'd be plagued by the man-in-a-suit syndrome, but we did what we could by adding arm extensions and hanging them on wires and doing a lot of shots upside-down and at different speeds. Also, as the title implies, there's lots more of everything.\" More both in quantity and form. Since the element of surprise had necessarily been blunted by Ridley Scott's original, Cameron wanted very much to have a trump card he could play during his final act. He found that card by mentally completing the alien's life cycle. In the first film it had been established that the alien eggs contain an organism capable of reflexively springing out and clamping itself onto the face of an unwary host. Thrusting an embryonic seed down its victim's throat, the face-hugger dies, its life essence imparted to the parasitic creature now growing inexorably within. Reaching sufficient maturity, the chestburster rips its way out of the host, continuing its rapid development into a full-size alien warrior. But what of the eggs? Where do they come from? The question had been addressed in the Alien script, and a sequence demonstrating the origin of the eggs had even been shot and incorporated into the workprint. It was cut shortly before release, however, because Ridley Scott felt that it unnecessarily slowed the pace of his carefully orchestrated finale. The deletion proved most fortunate for James Cameron. \"The version of cocooning that was shot for the first film involved the concept of the host's body slowly forming into the egg itself from which the face-hugger would ultimately emerge. Had that existed in the first film, my story wouldn't have worked. In my story, the eggs come from somewhere else. Ripley's the one who thinks about it and figures out that there's something missing, something they haven't seen yet. They've seen the eggs, they've seen the parasite that emerges from the eggs, they've seen the embryo laid by that parasite emerge from a host person, and they've seen the embryo grow up into a supposedly adult form. But that adult form - one of them, anyway - couldn't possibly have laid the thousand or so eggs that filled the inside of the derelict ship. At least that was my theory. So working from that image - acres and acres of these quite large eggs, two-and-a-half to three feet tall - I began to focus on the idea of a hierarchical hive structure where the central figure is a giant queen whose role it is to further the species.\" his course now set, Cameron had only to find the people who could bring his biomechanical nightmare to life. 'We started the film in late April, early May. At that point, nothing had been designed - literally zippo. I knew that the most critical thing was to get the designs fast, because you can't storyboard without them and you can't build anything without them and we were supposed you ant supposed to start shooting in September. The only way we could see getting the job done was to bring on two top designers and divide up the workload - with me taking on certain parts of it myself to fill in the gaps.\" The two designers enlisted for the project came from very different disciplines. Syd Mead, a major figure in industrial design, had contributed futuristic concepts for such films as Star Trek, Tron, Blade Runner and 2010. Ron Cobb, a noted illustrator and cartoonist, had similarly contributed to such projects as Star Wars, Alien, Conan the Barbarian and The Last Starfighter. \"Having to arbitrarily divide between the two, it seemed to me that Syd works best in areas that are very futuristic while Ron tends to create with elements that are a little more present-day recognizable. Syd is very thorough when it comes to designing things that can actually be constructed and work - that's his background - so I thought I'd let him handle the cutting edge technology, which I envisioned as being primarily the military stuff. As a result, he was responsible for most of the Sulaco designs - both the exterior for the miniature and the interior for the sets. To Ron I gave the colony because I figured the colony would have a more straightforward, rudimentary-typetechnology - whatever they couldafford. The end result was we all went design crazy for about a month.\" Within three days of his first contact from Jim Cameron, Syd Mead was already at his drawing board working on concepts for the Sulaco. In the script, Cameron's description of the spacecraft exterior left more than ample room for interpretation. In fact, all Mead really had to go on was a passing reference to metal spires slicing across the frame, followed by a mountain of steel. \"From my original reading of the script,\" said Mead. \"I interpreted this word picture as being something like a large melonshaped rocketship with a vast antenna field on the front of it. For camera purposes, however, Jim wanted more of a vertical blade format. That way, once the spires went past and the main bulk of the ship entered the frame, there'd be a moving wall of detail that wouldn't require you to move the camera or change the focus or anything. So I had to reorient my thinking a bit based on how he was planning to shoot it.\" In an instance of form following function, the final Sulaco"],["9","Aliens","concept - blocky in some sections, spindly in others - bore marked resemblance to a futuristic firearm, with a predominant cargo hold section in the center, a giant antenna array protruding from the front, and crew stations, living quarters and engine components in the rear. \"Essentially the idea was that it was a military load-carrying vessel - an enormous space freighter, if you will. In terms of profile - a lot of the tower structures and things - I used for inspiration some old aircraft carrier and battleship shapes, stacking all this stuff up. Down the side of the ship I put a long row of enormous, roll-up doors in big, overengineered heavy frames so that as the thing moves by very slowly you can see all the elaborate detail and the loading bays. In movies, you don't have to match everything up, inside and out, but there has to be a logical visual connection. I figured that with drop-ships mounted inside, we'd need a bay that was about a hundred feet wide, so that's what I used in approximating the scale for the rest of the ship, Behind the cargo bay were the big drive shrouds. In theory it was a fusion sphere of some sort that drove the engines to produce the thrust. Then at the very front, projecting forward, was this elaborate antenna array that looked like you could call the next universe if you pushed the right button. So it had to look very elaborate, it had to look gigantic and it had to have this highly articulated finished appearance to it. And a lot of ins and outs so it would not look like a solid object covered with detail - you could see through different parts and in and around suspended pods and things.\" While Syd Mead labored in relative seclusion, Ron Cobb's initial involvement entailed a series of brainstorming sessions with Jim Cameron. \"I didn't see it exactly as science fiction,\" said Cobb. \"To me, it was kind of a stylized fantasy on technology - almost satirical in a way - as was the first one. The original film had people trapped in a machine - a metaphor for the industrial world, perhaps - but the inherent elements of humor were mostly submerged. Aliens struck me right away as a grand takeoff on Vietnam, with all of these odd echoings of Apocalypse Now or something. There was a rich, witty aspect to the picture. It's not a projection, truly, into the future. It's more like a contemporary war film. From the vehicles to the weapons to the patches on their uniforms, it's more a reflection of Vietnam than it is the future.\" For reasons of economy, it was decided that England would be the best place to shoot the ambitious sequel. \"Not only would it cost us less,\" said Gale Hurd, \"but there seemed to be such a continuity in respect to the film in England. Alien, of course, was shot there, and numerous technicians who had worked on it - and who had since risen to greater heights - were still around and available to work for us in more senior capacities. Not that we were trying to recreate what had been seen in the first film - ours was to be very much different - but that sense of continuity, we felt, would be an intangible asset.\" Hurd and Cameron set up shop at Pinewood Studios in July and began assembling their key production staff members. Among the first to be hired was production designer Peter Lamont - a two-time CINEFEX 27 • 9 IDEA ROR"],["10","Aliens","10 • CINEFEY 27"],["11","Aliens","Oscar nominee with four James Bond films to his credit - whose job it would be to translate the graphic brainstorms of Syd Mead and Ron Cobb into functional sets and props. Also enlisted was director of photography Adrian Biddle who had come up through the ranks of commercial cinematography at Ridley Scott Associates and had worked as a focus puller on Alien. Approached to supervise the extensive floor effects required for the film was John Richardson, who had among his credits The Omen, Superman and three Bond films. Richardson had been involved early-on with the original Allen - when walter Hill was still planning to direct - but to his subsequent regret had withdrawn in the face of repeated postponements. Eager to make self-restitution for having missed out on a classic, Richardson read the Aliensscript and promptly signed on. The remaining two-thirds of the effects would be supervised by Americans - past associates of Hurd and Cameron who had proven themselves capable of quality work under less than nominal conditions. Stan Winston, who had engineered the robot endoskeleton for The Terminator, was already hard at work on the creature effects - a massive assignment that would require eight months and a crew of forty to complete. For miniatures and opticals, the producer-director team selected L.A. Effects Group, a fledgling optical house that had recently finished work on Creature - ironically, a thinly-disguised variation on the Alien theme. \"Obviously,\" said Cameron, \"we could have gone to Boss Film or Industrial Light and Magic - one of the elite facilities - but we didn't know those people personally and it occurred to us that since I like to get directly involved in everything that perhaps that might not be the best way to go. They do, after all, have a tendency to want to do things their own way. The results are stupendous, of course, and I look forward to working with one of them on a picture someday, but I just wasn't sure about this one - especially in light of our decision to do the postproduction in England. So Gale and I began looking around to see who else could and would and should do the effects for this film, and what attracted us most to L.A. Effects Group was the fact that Bob Skotak and Dennis Skotak came with the package. I'd worked with them on a number of films back in my effects days and I liked them a lot. Both have an incredible eye for the work and I felt they would serve very well as an interface between me and the all-British effects crew and shooting unit.\" \"I think the reason Jim wanted to work with us,\" commented Bob Skotak, \"is our orientation towards producing effects images in-camera, which is especially important if you're doing any process work. Jim is not very fond of bluescreen because it tends to set up shots that look like special effects shots. They tend to be very smooth, and if there's a pan it's normally a very steady pan. In essence, bluescreen dictates a sort of slick style instead of the nitty-gritty, hit-the-ground-running approach that Jim seems to favor. For that reason, Jim prefers process work - front projection and back projection - because it allows for more random camera movement and can be done in camera. The thing about it, though, is that you have to generate your plates during production. There's no sticking a blue screen back there and worrying about what you're going to do with it later. Whatever s supposed to be happening behind the actors has to be right there on the screen during principal photography, so the effects team has to work right alongside the main unit and somehow keep one step ahead.\" Cameron's bias toward effects techniques many of his contemporaries consider outdated was also in evidence in the area of miniature photography. Though motion control would play a role, Cameron sought wherever possible to use large-scale models in miniature settings and environments that could be recorded directly onto original negative. Since process plates were needed up front - most of them involving miniatures of one sort or another - a massive modelbuilding initiative was clearly in order. \"As it turned out,\" said miniatures technical supervisor Pat McClung, \"we had to build almost everything at once, right at the beginning. Not only did Jim need most of our models for the process plates, but there was also lots of video material needed for monitors inside the drop-ship and the armored personnel carrier.\" Indeed, it was largely through process material, video scanners and straightout miniature shots that the planet Acheron and its tiny earth colony would come into being on the Pinewood soundstages. From the moment the colonial marines drive their armored personnel carrier into the belly of the drop-ship shuttlecraft and, stowed within it, plummet to the planet surface, the film unfolds in a world of models and miniatures. In fact, only one exterior set - representing the main accessway into the colony - would be constructed full-size. The remainder of the planetary environment - from drop-ship landing field to colony complex to giant atmosphere processing station nearby - would exist solely in miniature form. Ultimately it was the drop-ship and the armored personnel carrier - functionallywell-established, but as yet undesigned - that would determine an optimal scale for the rest of the model work. At one-twelfth and one-sixth actual size, respectively, each would be small enough to maneuver without significant difficulty, yet large enough to hold depth of field and simulate an appropriate sense of mass. The vehicles, both airborne and surface, would not be operating in a vacuum, however. Each required one or more sets in which to function - and at the onesixth scale, some of them were very large indeed. \"Much of what was built full-scale we had to duplicate in miniature,\" McClung continued. \"Peter Lamont and his people built part of the front section of the colony as a full-size set so the APC could drive up and the troopers disembark. All the other shots of it driving through the town were done in miniature on a set that was about eighty feet long. We needed a set that large because of the sixthscale APC and the other vehicles. Anything smaller than that and you risk having your models look like models.\" Regrettably, the colony complex had the distinction of being both the largest miniature constructed for the film and the one least in evidence in the final release. As written and shot, the To accommodate Cameron's photographic needs, Mead reconfigured the Sulaco into a flattened blade-like shape with fusion reactor and thrusters in the rear, an expansive cargo hold section amidship and a forwarddirected antenna array. / Miniatures technical supervisor Pat McClung adds a few final details to the sixfoot Sulaco. As with many of the models employed in the film, the Sulaco was assembled in rough form by outside contractor Peter Astin, then detailed and painted at the studio. Featured in only a half-dozen shots, the warship - when finished - was transported to Arkadon Motion Control and photographed by postproduction effects supervisor Brian Johnson and his crew. CINEFEX 27 • 11"],["12","Aliens","constructed on D stage at Pinewood Studios. Derived from a concept by Syd Mead and translated into threeproduction designer Peter Lamont, the massive set had feature a full-size mockup of down to the planet surface. / Another significant artifact in the cargo hold was the controlled walking forklift Richardson and his physical complete cargo hold was too large to be built full-size, a twelfth-scale miniature was constructed for establishing shuttlecraft launch scenes. forward ship in preparation The Sulaco cargo hold as dimensional reality by as its most dominant the drop-ship used to shuttle the assault team power-loader - a servoconstructed by John effects team. / Since the shots and also for the Bob Skotak adjusts the for the first launch. 12 * CINEFEX 27 film was to have cut direct to Acheron from the scene on Gateway station where Ripley discovers that her planet of horrors has been colonized by sixty unsuspecting families. A brief montage of shots as the terraforming complex prepares for a storm would have established the ruggedness of the locale and the fortitude of its inhabitants. Then the action was to shift to a remote sector of the planet where the Jorden family - independent prospectors and surveyors - has been sent, without warning of possible danger, to look for Ripley's derelict spacecraft. Both sequences - ultimately cut for time - were designed to rely almost exclusively on miniatures. \"In deciding how we were going to establish the colony complex,\" said Bob Skotak, \"one of our first thoughts after reading the script was that we would do matte paintings of the whole place with some wind and dust blowing across in front of them. But that wouldn't have been very satisfying, What we really needed was to get right down into the streets and show everyday activities going on - show a windstorm and have people battening down the hatches and lashing tarps onto vehicles and things like that. We realized it would have been prohibitively expensive to build a set like that for real; but by building perspective miniatures and using beam-splitter photography, we figured we could integrate live-action elements into small-scale sets and have it all look convincing.\" As conceived and rendered by Ron Cobb, the Acheron colony was to feature three distinct components. First was the main complex, a small cluster of prefabricated buildings housing all scientific and medical facilities. Second, rising from the planet surface a mile or so away, was a monstrous atmosphere processing station installed to help tame the inhospitable planet and make its naturally poisonous air suitable for breathing. Third was an anomalous adjunct to the neat and precisely configured main area - asprawling conglomeration of makeshift structures offering recreation and respite unavailable within the central complex. \"I thought the main section of the colony should be very modular,\" Cobb explained, \"so I designed two basic modules - long hexagonal cylinder-type things that fit together in various ways and could be transported intact, lowered into place from some sort of hovercraft and then mounted on huge concrete piers. I thought that look would convey the temporary quality of the place. The idea behind the ramshackle boomtown was that it developed pretty much as an afterthought. Everything was to look reminiscent of cargo containers that had been turned into buildings, so there are a lot of ribbed metal boxes sitting on hydraulic jacks with various interconnecting cables of one sort or another. Then we came up with a big storm wall that would surround the colony to keep out the blowing dirt or drifting ash or whatever it was supposed to be on this ridiculous planet.\" The scientific sections of the colony were the easiest to design and construct. \"The buildings were all modular,\" explained Pat McClung, \"so we were able to use a lot of repeating sections. Bob Skotak and I worked out what areas of the complex would be seen during the shooting and what faces would actually have to be built. At that point we called in the chargehand - who in England is the construction supervisor on the stage - and he brought in some of his carpenters who made the basic structures out of wood. The outer surfaces were just skins that were vacuformed over patterns made in the model shop. These vacuformed sheets were then applied to the wooden framework, which was fairly easy.\" All that remained then was to detail the outer surfaces and dress the surrounding areas. \"Jim wanted sort of a high-tech complex that had been battered mercilessly by the environment of the planet,\" said Bob Skotak, \"very rusted, very beat up, very aged - far beyond what had normally been done in these kinds of films. In his spare time, he'd walk around the studio taking endless Polaroids of rusted doors and things. He's a specific director - very specific. So you'd get this Polaroid and he'd say, 'That's exactly the kind of rust spot I want.' Lots of times, rather than paint in the rust, we'd go after the real thing. At the studio there was a big dump by the 007 stage where virtually everything is burned as soon as they're done with it - sets, lights, everything. So there we'd be on a Sunday afternoon rooting around for choice bits of sixthscale junk, finding all this neat stuff like tin cans and cables and metal sheets that were rusted out - anything that could conceivably fit into our set - and then hauling it all back to the stage in buckets. It was certainly much cheaper than manufacturing everything from scratch.\" Much of the precious junk would find its way into the makeshift frontier town. \"A lot of the buildings were put together with bits ponte tow rom the pad bee and pieces from the prop shed,\" said McClung, \"where a guy named Mark Harris had been collecting everything from discarded plastic computer cases to chunks of jet aircraft. One day while I was wandering around back there I found a couple of airline cargo containers - the kind they pile luggage into and then load onto 747s or other large aircraft. One of them was particularly interesting. At one time it had had nice corrugated aluminum sides, but in its present state it was totally beaten up and smashed. I thought it might make a nice building, so I took some Polaroids of it and showed them to Bob and he drew in some pipes and other odds and ends, and that became our bar. Three of the other buildings were made from polyurethane baskets that are used in England for carrying beer. Those were bolted together and Bob came up with some ideas on how they could be detailed. We had large numbers of plastic TV backs that were injection molding cast-offs - in odd colors like pink or blue - and these were all glued on. Then we made small doors and overhanging lamps. We also did a lot of vacuforming on that set, mostly from a whole library of shapes that the plaster shop had - everything from rocks to futuristic-looking panels.\" \"We're not talking technical prophecy here,\" said Cobb. \"It was understood from the very beginning that what we wanted was not a serious consideration of a believable future, but rather something more stylized. Jim, basically, was interested in the kind of phony engineering that I do - quickly coming up with a drawing or an idea of what such-and-such might look like. So"],["13","Aliens","CINEFEX 27 • 13"],["14","Aliens","that's what I gave him. My feeling is you can either make a game try at predicting the future or you can divert with the realization that we all experience the future as it comes to us. And for me, it's almost more powerful to evoke a future with a lot of funny quirks in it than it is to predict in a very linear way what might actually be. Inevitably, that becomes very narrow - and it's probably no more accurate in the long run. What was important, really, was that the designs have a strong visual and dramatic component. If we were being serious about all this, it isn't likely that the colonists would even try to build on a planet surface like that. They'd probably go underground. But if we'd done that, we'd have missed out on dusty streets and people huddled against the wind - the real frontier western look.\" Because of its overall length, the colony street set had to be laid out diagonally on one of the Pinewood soundstages. Even at that, some of the buildings in the rear had to be constructed in forced perspective. Fully assembled, the main complex and the makeshift boomtown flanked each other, divided by a dirt road that led off toward the atmosphere processing station. \"For flyover shots,\" McClung continued, \"we also built a smaller colony that was about eight-foot square, with all the buildings and modules and so forth. That was used primarily for the monitor footage which we shot directly on videotape rather than doing it on film and then transferring it. There was even a smaller one at one point - a very quick one. The art department had made up a little wooden building block model for Jim so he could play around with it and lay out the basic configuration of the colony. We took that, painted it black, added some lights and set it far off in the distance - and it worked just fine.\" The smallest of the colony miniatures was used as a distant element in conjunction with the atmosphere processing station - another priority project for the miniatures crew. \"The AP station started out looking pretty much like a volcano,\" Cobb commented. \"That was an idea Jim and I both shared. He did a lot of drawings. I did a lot of drawings, Ultimately, I think it was more his concept than mine.\" Cameron's primary concern was that it be made to look big. \"Ron's design was basically just a conical shape,\" said Cameron, \"and I knew from experience - having worked on a film for Roger Corman called Galaxy of Terror where one of the main objects was a pyramid - that the approach to something with walls that slant back like that basically defies perspective from ground level. As a result, it becomes very hard to create a sense of height. To get around that problem, I added some outward radiating fins to give it scale so that the station would come forward in perspective as we approached it.\" Since the atmosphere processing station was separated from the colony by about a mile and would only be seen by itself, it was not necessary to construct it to any particular scale. Thus, a six-foot model was constructed to represent the thousand-foot-tall facility. While the model shop toiled away, Peter Lamont and his crew were engaged elsewhere transforming five large soundstages 14 • CINEFEX 27"],["15","Aliens","into full-size sets. Only one - the Narcissus interior - would be a direct carryover from the first film. As scripted, the emergency shuttlecraft in which Ripley had stood her ground and defeated the alien marauder would this time be needed for an opening scene involving the tiny vessel's interception and recovery by a deep-space salvage team. The original set, of course, had long ago been struck - even the plans had vanished - so Lamont and company were given the task of recreating it from photographs and frame enlargements. Tiny by comparison with those going up around it, the Narcissus set was completed early in the construction phase and slated for the first day of principal photography. Shooting the opening first would afford Cameron the opportunity to begin the film in sequence, with a scene that was comfortably straightforward and uninvolved, and at the same time give his construction unit some badly needed additional time to finish the more ambitious sets still to come. As it turned out, however, the Narcissuswould be the very last thing to go before the camera. A ripple of panic swept through the Aliens production office when word arrived that Half Moon Street, then being shot across town at EMI Elstree, was running seriously behind schedule - and Sigourney Weaver was starring in it. Since Weaver was to have segued directly from Half Moon Street to Aliens, she would now be unavailable for the first three weeks of principal photography. Aliens' carefully laid out shooting schedule was instantly obsolete. Only a few sequences could be tackled without her. Of those, the one most suitable for advancement was the assault team's foray into the atmosphere processing station which has been annexed by the aliens and converted into an eerie biomechanical nesting place. Since the sequence involved an elaborate set with a complex hanging miniature, a full-size armored personnel carrier, high-tech automatic weapons and flame-throwers, an alien chest-burster and a swarm of murderous warriors, the rescheduling decision would impact on nearly everyone across the board. Complicating things further, it was the only sequence slated to be shot on location. The decision to go off the Pinewood lot had been an early one. Rather than build an expansive industrial-looking complex at the studio and then overlay it with organic alien-produced forms, it seemed preferable to employ an actual industrial site for the basic structure. After looking at oil refineries and deserted gasworks, Peter Lamont and his location crew selected a decommissioned electrical generating station in the London suburb of Acton, about fourteen miles from the studio. 'With certain reservations,\" said Lamont, \"it looked like a viable proposition. It was very large and full of machinery, with tremendous stairways and grillwork floors. The grillwork was especially nice since it allowed us to see through from one story to the next; and with the smoky atmosphere Jim likes to use, it gave everything a kind of bleeding light effect. The main problem was that this had been a coal-fired generating station and there was asbestos all over the place. Since the plant had been derelict for several years, things were starting to rot and asbestos fibers were in the air - which, of course, is very hazardous, So the first thing we had to do was to get a company that specializes in such work to come in and remove all the asbestos and make the place sate. While that was going on, we had a whole team of modelers back at the studio doing pieces for the alien structure.\" As with all aspects of the film, Jim Cameron had both a specific look and a specific design rationale for the biomechanical forms that were to be retrofitted over the existing machinery and structures inside the Acton power plant. \"The alien structure,\" Cameron explained, \"provided an interesting opportunity for us to do a Gigeresque-type structure created biologically by the aliens - much the way ants do when they cement the walls of their tunnels using saliva mixed with granules of rock and sand.\" Swirling amorphous shapes - organic but intermixed with forms suggestive of bones, machinery parts and flexible conduit - were sculpted in clay at Pinewood and then used to generate molds from which multiple castings could be produced. \"We got two castings a day from each mold,\" said Lamont. \"Some were cast off in fiberglass and others were vacuformed. In all there were hundreds of pieces, most of which were painted by one of our scenic artists. We spent about three weeks on that while the power station was being cleaned up. Then we moved into the plant and started fitting these things into place. A team of painters had already gone through with I don't know how many gallons of silver spray paint, so already it was starting to look not much like a power station. By the time we finished, it was really transformed. We had just three weeks to complete the work once we got inside. It was quite a chore. We started on the lower floor and were still working on the upper floor when production began. As Jim came up shooting, we were gradually retreating Though the alienized areas were extensive, Cameron wanted to introduce the setting with a shot that would have been all but impossible to achieve without some camera trickery. As the marines enter the alien chamber, the camera was to pan down the giant multistory structure completely encrusted with the resinous alien secretion. \"It would have taken forever to do that for real,\" said Bob Skotak, \"not to mention the expense. So after thinking about it for a while, we decided the best way to do it would be with a hanging miniature. The way the shot was set up was that the actors would be very close, literally right on the same plane as the hanging miniature - just barely getting their heads underneath it - and then they'd kind of look around and walk back into the depth of the set. So what we needed to do was to continue the encrustation up above the actors, and also continue the pipe work and the scaffolding and the catwalks and everything else. The art department had gone to Acton and gotten all the measurements - where the lift was, where the pipes were, basically the whole floor plan. Then, to save time while the plant was being cleaned up, they reconstructed that section in plywood on L stage and built the cocoon mass over it in carved styrofoam. While it was there on the stage, Dennis Though it was Cameron's intent from the first that the drop-ship look and perform like a helicopter gunship without rotors, an approved design was slow in coming. Syd Mead based his concept on a Sikorsky H-53 heavy-lift helicopter modified with vertical takeoff and landing capability and vectored thrust. Caterpillar treads afforded limited ground mobility. Ron Cobb rendered several versions of a Bell UH-IE hybrid equipped with large outrigger armament pods. The final design - derived from a foam-core mockup constructed by Jim Cameron - most closely resembled a McDonnell Douglas AH-64 helicopter reconfigured with weapons arrayed in a variable geometry configuration. All three designs were equipped with internal cargo space sufficient to carry an armored personnel carrier. CINEFEX 27 • 15"],["16","Aliens","The drop-ship makes a low twelfth-scale miniatures. / ships for an approach to the principal flying rig consisted top from which the model attached to the ship at four points and were controllable mechanism that could rotate the entire vessel or raise and movement was achieved by pushing the platform along the tracks. Anywhere from pass over the deserted Acheron colony. As with most of the shuttlecraft scenes, the overflight sequence was achieved largely in-camera with Effects technicians ready one of the six-foot dropcolony landing pad. The of an elevated, trackmounted platform with a twelve-foot boom arm on was suspended. Wires via a steering wheel lower its nose. Linear four to eight operators were required for each model move. 16 • CINEFEX 27 and I set up a camera in a position we'd selected while at Acton and determined all the measurements we'd need for the hanging miniature and its supports. Then we ran some film through the camera of the beginning, middle and end of a tilt-down, made prints and traced on top of them where we wanted all the lines and pipes and everything to go. We gave that sketch to Steve Begg and he went up to Acton with Chrissy Overs, and together they finished off the miniature on site.\" The miniature - about ten feet high and twelve feet wide - required about two weeks to complete in basic form. Then came the laborious task of developing a foolproof blend with the fullsize structure beyond. \"There were a lot of last-minute adjustments to be made,\" explained Dennis Skotak, \"mainly because the live-action set was not quite ready until a day or so before we started to shoot. Since a lot of what we had to do depended on the final set dressing and paint job, it was pretty crazy those last couple of days trying to get our blend just right.\" Complicating matters was the fact that Cameron planned to shoot the sequence in fairly dense smoke. Since the success of the hanging miniature depended largely upon the camera's two-dimensional perception of its false perspective as being true, a uniform infusion of smoke would have created an inconsistency in aerial density. The miniature - supposedly the background imagery - should logically have been more diffused by the smoke, but since it was in fact closer to the camera than the foreground material, the actual diffusion would have been less. To avoid this problem it became necessary to cheat the lighting and pump additional smoke in over the miniature. \"It was a very delicate balance. We found as we were there that by adding the slightest amount of fill light on the miniature and then wetting everything down and blowing in just the right amount of fog that it all worked together. It was transitory, though. Cast and crew would be standing around waiting, and when everything looked right, we'd say: 'That's it! Shoot!' - and everybody would go for it. The blend was there literally just for moments and then it would be gone. We were very fortunate that after all the struggle it worked.\" For the effects crew, the successful completion of the hanging miniature shot - in the very first week of principal photography - was a major cause for celebration. Their elation turned to anxiety, however, when two weeks into principal photography actor James Remar - originally cast in the key role of Hicks - was replaced by Michael Biehn. \"Jim Cameron came in on a Saturday and said, 'I think we're going to have to do that hanging miniature over again,\" Dennis Skotak continued. \"The way the shot had been staged, Hicks was very close to the camera and the actor playing him was no longer in the picture. We sweated bullets all weekend. Not only was the shot a bitch, but the set had been struck and the miniature - which was too fragile to be moved - had been destroyed in the process. Fortunately, Jim and the editor figured out a way to cut the scene so we didn't have to do it over - but for a few hours there, it was hell.\" Inside the alien structure, the marines find physical confirmation of the colonists' fates. Stuffed into corners and cocooned in the resinous organic material from which the structure itself is fashioned, the least fortunate of the human victims are discovered in tortured repose - lifeless shells with gaping chest wounds, destroyed insidiously from within by the alien parasites. One of the colonists is still alive. The foreign lifeform growing within her has not yet wrenched itself free and she pleads with the troopers to kill her before it does. Only moments later she screams as the chest-burster erupts from her torso. Though most of the cocooned colonists were inanimate body sculptures topped with life-castings of faces grotesquely contorted in pain, the still-living woman required a major effects setup. Stan Winston and his crew were intimately involved, even in the set design and construction. \"For the chest-burster scene, we built the set so that the woman - Barbara Coles - was leaning slightly forward, cocooned between a couple of pillars. The lower part of her torso - from the waist down - was a fiberglass duplicate of her body surrounded by all this cocoon stuff. She could thus lay right up into that form and the upper part of her body would be real and the lower part would be fiberglass. That was the configuration at first, when she speaks. Then, for the scene where the chest-burster comes out, she was on a slant board with a foam rubber appliance from the neck down. So it was her own head and arms, an appliance neck and torso and the fiberglass shell below the waist.\" A similar setup would be featured earlier in the film during the Gateway sequence, though it would actually be shot much later. Haunted by her memories and harassed by disbelieving investigators, Ripley dreams that like Kane she has fallen victim to the alien parasites. \"It's actually a wonderful effect. She pulls her top up and you see her whole body stretch as the chest-burster pushes out from the inside. For that particular gimmick, Sigourney was on a slant board under the bed with a duplicate appliance body on top and an operator underneath pushing the chest-burster up. It was particularly effective because anyone who's seen the first film knows exactly what it is, but it never actually bursts through. Tom Woodruff was largely responsible for that effect.\" The full chest-burster effect - on screen for only a few seconds - required two separate versions of the creature. \"The only thing we added to the Alien chest-burster was a pair of tiny little arms that folded up very tightly against the body,\" explained Winston. \"We thought the chest-burster ought to have them to help pull itself free - and, after all, the big one it grows into has arms. To maintain continuity, we made our chest-burster look exactly like the original when it first emerges. Then these little arms come out and work. Of course, the scene and the moment are so dramatic that only the most discerning eye will even notice, but we thought they ought to be there. Tony Gardner did the basic chest-burster sculpture while we were still in the States, and Bill Sturgeon produced the mechanics for the one that was actually used to burst through the chest. It had a very strong metal structure and cables that were used to provide its various movements. That was about as far as we'd gotten by the"],["17","Aliens","time we left for England. All the molds and the mechanics and the skins were sent ahead, and over there a second articulated chest-burster was developed by Steve Norrington. That one had even more life than the first - mainly because it didn't have to push through anything so it didn't have to be as strong.\" Curiously, one of the major traumas involved in executing the chest-burster effect for the first film - getting the victim's shirt to tear appropriately and on cue - proved to be just as much a dilemma for the second. \"There was no problem getting the chest-burster through the body,\" said Winston. \"The foam rubber was hollowed out and the skin was prescored and the chestburster itself was built on a big metal harness that fit onto one of our operators' arms. All we needed was for him to punch it through from below. But we had a helluva time trying to get the shirt to do exactly what it was supposed to. Luckily we had about six of them. They were all pre-distressed so they would tear easily, but throughout the takes there were times when the chest-burster wouldn't make a clean exit or blood would hit the shirt before the breakthrough or something like that. It was rough on everybody, psychologically and otherwise. So much thought and energy went into that whole sequence that to sit there on the set thinking you had all your bases covered and have the T-shirts, ofall things, not react the way they're supposed to - it was frustrating. Jim was going crazy and I was going After the chest-burster breaks through - killing its host in the process - the troopers open up on it with flame-throwers. \"For the last scene, we built a complete duplicate of the actress in her death position - head, body, everything - which we then put into the set. At the same time, we replaced the original chestburster with the one Steve Norrington had made and had it writhing around and really going crazy. The movement was stupendous - that little sucker was really alive!\" When all was ready and the desired action achieved, Cameron gave a signal and the whole setup was torched. \"We built all the flame-throwers from scratch,\" said John Richardson, \"because they had to be totally self-contained, even in terms of the fuel they fired. And obviously, they had to be very carefully designed since the artists had to carry them and use them. After we built our first one, we took it up to the art department so they could come up with a cosmetic shell for the outside of it which we then dressed onto the gun. In the end, we built a whole range of them. Depending on which one we used, we could get a jet of flame anywhere from forty-five feet down to five feet. It was a bit like playing golf - you had to pick the right club for the right hole. And again, depending on how far we wanted it to fire and what we wanted to see happen when it hit at the other end, we had a choice of two different fuels. Most of the time we used propane gas which tends to use itself up in the air as it is being fired. If we wanted to catch something on fire, however, we used an alcohol gasoline mixture. That mixture would remain in liquid form as it went through the air and would then set fire to whatever it hit. From a safety CINEFEX 27 • 17"],["18","Aliens","18 - CINEFEX 27"],["19","Aliens","point of view - and also for control - I normally prefer the propane. Then, if you want something to catch on fire, you cover the area you want with a flammable gel and ignite it. What we use is like rubber cement. It's fairly volatile, but it doesn't normally harm whatever's underneath it because it forms a skin of rubber over the surface. And since it's an adhesive, you can paint it onto anything and know exactly where it's going to burn and how much it's going to burn. So there's much more control. You get an artist running around with the liquid and you can set half your set on fire before you know what's happened.\" Shaken by what they have seen, the troopers have only a moment to recover before an onslaught of alien warriors affords them more than ample opportunity to exercise the rest of their high-tech weaponry. But high-tech - at least for Jim Cameron - did not mean the raygun-style rotoscope and pyrotechnic effects that have become near-standard in post-Star Warsscience fiction films. Instead, he wanted something heavier and altogether more formidable — solidly based in real-world technology, but embellished with futuristic capabilities and design elements. Indeed, though most of the armament bore little resemblance to anything contemporary, all the essential pieces were adapted from existing firearms. Standard issue for the colonial marines was the 'pulse rifle,' a dual function weapon capable of firing both machine gun rounds and grenades. Also on hand was the 'smart-gun, a larger automatic weapon affixed to a floating body harnessand targeted via thermal gun sights mounted within the operator's helmet. \"We spent a long time choosing guns that we could adapt to look like something nobody'sever seen before,\" said Richardson. \"Jim is very much into powerful-looking weaponry, so whatever we came up with had to have a lot of guts to it. Specifically, we wanted guns that gave the biggest, brightest muzzle flash we could get - with a minimum amount of smoke since smoke tends to make things look like fireworks going off.\" Selected for the pulse rifle was a Thompson submachine gun coupled with a Franchi pump-action shotgun mechanism for firing high-impact mini-grenades. A Spandau MG-42 machine gun was chosen for the smart-gun and adapted to fit a modified Steadicam harness. \"It was very much a build-as-you-go situation,\" Richardson continued. \"For the smart-guns, we took the two Steadicam harnesses we'd rented, reworked them a bit and mounted our machine guns on the end. I figured the guns were going to end up being a lot heavier than a standard Arriflex camera: but they weren't, so we had to add some extra weight to them to get a perfect balance. Once the guns were mounted, we had to work out how one could best handle and control them. We then put handles on in the appropriate positions and turned everything over to the art department to be redressed - bearing in mind, of course, the complex functions of machine guns feeding rounds in and spent cases out. It was a laborious process, but we got through it. You'd have to be an absolute gun fanatic to recognize any of them.\" Also constructed for the film, but ultimately relegated to the cutting room floor, were 'sentry guns.' Unmanned, fully automatic weapons mounted on tripods, the sentry guns were programmed to scan a given field of fire and shoot at anything that entered. \"We had to build special hydraulic tripods, remote-controlled so the sentry guns would seem to pan and tilt on their own. And they had to be very strongly constructed since we had real machine guns popping away on the top of them at six hundred rounds a minute.\" Wooden blanks were used in the smart-guns and sentry weapons. \"Whenever I mention wooden blanks,\" said Richardson, \"people tend to throw up their arms in horror. I guess all they can picture are these little wooden bullets coming out at them. But in actuality, they're very safe. In all our automatic blankfiring weapons, there are baffles in the gun barrel that shatter the blanks into tiny little splinters so that by the time they get to the end they've been completely consumed by the heat of the powder burning.\" Despite their formidable weaponry, the once-cocky marines are quickly routed by the unarmed alien guerrillas that descend upon them from places of perfect concealment within their resin-encrusted stronghold. The lightning-paced sequence - the first of two major battles and a flurry of skirmishes - was, for Stan Winston and his crew, the culmination of four months' worth of frantic effort geared toward producing what would seem to be an army of alien warriors. Cameron had convinced him it was possible. \"I think if you can do one, you can do any number,\" said Cameron. \"Anyone who's been through the process of creating a creature effect, or a character that's sculpted in clay and molded and blown and painted or whatever, knows that to make one takes six months and to make two takes six months and a couple of extra days. I'm exaggerating slightly, but there's an economy of scale there. It does create additional problems when you're shooting, though. All the things that can go wrong with one creature go wrong five times as often with five creatures. On the other hand, you have five times as much to look at so your attention is a bit divided.\" As with the chest-burster, minor design changes were effected. \"On the original alien,\" Cameron continued, \"there was a translucent cowl covering the whole top of the head that looked kind of like a porpoise back. We planned to do the same thing with ours, and to that end Stan had Tom Woodruff sculpt up a ribbed, bone-like understructure that would fit underneath and be slightly visible through the cowl. When it was finished, they gave K and they ge it a real nice paint job and I took a look at it and said, 'Hey, this looks much more interesting the way it is. So we pitched the cowl and decided that this was just another generation of aliens - slightly mutated.\" Other alterations were so minute as to be virtually unnoticeable. \"It was all quite subtle,\" said Winston, \"Details that were obviously tacked onto the first one - little hoses and things - we worked at in a sculptural way so that the organic and inorganic elements blended together better. It's nothing you could ever detect on film - just the kind of thing you do to keep from getting bored duplicating exactly what someone else has already done.\" The Acheron colony as rendered by conceptual designer Ron Cobb. / Since any attempt to build the complex to scale would have been prohibitively expensive, only a portion of the main entryway was constructed full-size. The remainder of the colony exteriors would be conveyed solely with miniatures. For monitors inside the dropship, a relatively small-scale version of the colony was fabricated and then photographed in lowresolution video for on-set playback during the flyby. / A much larger, sixth-scale representation of the colony - stretching across an entire soundstage - was constructed for ground-level shots. Modular buildings comprising the main complex were hexagonal wooden frames covered with vacuformed skins, while the ramshackle boomtown nearby was cobbled together from assorted containers and existing artifacts. Kinetic elements included smoke and wind effects, falling rain and radio controlled vehicles. CINEFEX 27 • 19"],["20","Aliens","A major sequence deleted from the film was to have featured Newt Jorden and her family rediscovering the Skotak lines up a shot on a radio-controlled miniature of photographing through a beam-splitter, a painting of the derelict - positioned 90 degrees to the camera - was made to appear as though it were looming up behind the terrain. / For economy, only tractor was mocked up in section of the alien craft was used for a perspective shot actors well to the rear of the model and carefully aligning them through a crack in the hull, large amounts of light and a deep-focus lens made them appear to be standing in the foreground looking up derelict spacecraft. Bob the Jorden tractor. By miniature vehicle and the front portion of the full size. / A fifth-scale of the Jorden family standing outside the derelict. By placing the at the ship. 20 • CINEFEX 27 The one aspect of the alien warrior that Cameron and Winston would most liked to have changed they could not. \"I loved Alien,\" said Winston, \"it was probably my favorite horror movie of the decade. But if there was anything that I was disappointed in, it was at the very end when the alien gets blown out of the ship and you realize at last that what you've been waiting to see all this time is simply a man in a suit - a great suit, but a suit nonetheless. I found that very disheartening in the movie, and even more disheartening going into Aliens knowing that millions of people had seen this thing and therefore knew exactly what the alien looked like. Our hands were tied. We had to be true to the original.\" Being true to the original did not, however, mean emphasizing its weaknesses. Despite its being much more prominently featured than in the first film, Cameron was able to avoid even a single instance where the alien warrior looked obviously like a man in a suit. \"What Jim wanted were movements that were sporadic and odd and strange, so that even though they were men in suits, they didn't move like men in suits. So the big thing for us was to figure out a way to make these guys move and act in ways that were unlike a human - hanging from ceilings, jumping from wall to wall, doing insect-like moves and so on. The alien in the first film could never have done these things because it was a full rubber suit and was very difficult to move around in. To avoid that problem, we had to come up with an alternative design that allowed for great freedom of movement. We did that by eliminating the rubber suit aspect altogether and using instead black leotards with lightweight foam pieces attached to them. If you were to look at them hanging on a rack, you'd think, 'My God, those are just black leotards with pieces of stuff on them.' But when you see them in the film and they're wet and slimy, you can't tell the difference at all between ours and the original - and ours had complete freedom of movement.\" To best take advantage of that freedom of movement, stuntmen and dancers were engaged to play the aliens. \"Even though there were supposed to be hundreds of them, the original plan was never to have more than six in a shot at the same time. Allowing for spares, we actually did about twelve. Tom Woodruff and John Rosengrant supervised our suit construction team which included Julian Caldow, Nigel Booth, Lindsay McGowan and David Keen.\" \"For Alien, \"Cameron commented, \"they went out of their way to find a very tall person to be inside the suit - Bolaji Badejo was something like seven feet tall. We knew right off that we weren't going to be able to find ten people who were seven feet tall. On the other hand, in studying Alien we found that there was really only one shot in the entire film that shows a direct scale relationship between the creature and a human being, In all the other shots, it exists separately in the frame. So we decided that rather than go for height, we'd go for people who had the right physique to be in the suits - the thinnest people we could find that had the strength to do the kinds of movements we wanted, such as hanging on wires and crawling upside-down and that sort of thing. In the end, they averaged under six feet tall, but by putting them on footstools or doing low-angles on the creatures and high-angles on the people looking at them we were able to create the impression that the warriors were much taller than they actually were.\" To further disguise the fact that the basic alien warrior was a man in a suit, Winston and his crew constructed a half-dozen larger-than-life puppets. \"Whenever we could, we used one of the puppets because they were about eight feet tall and very puppete thin - there was no way they could have been humans in suits. A couple of them were rod- and cable-actuated and we could put their arms and legs into positions that a human couldn't get into. Others were floppy puppets that were just jointed so they could be thrown or crushed or blown up - whatever was needed. We also built a completely articulated upper torso puppet that had moveable lips and a mechanical tongue that came out with its own set of biting jaws. Then we did a lot of specific things for specific shots. We developed one full-size puppet specifically to be crushed by the armored personnel carrier - when Ripley drives over it - and then another torso for a tighter shot of the same thing.\" The armored personnel carrier - a fortified command-andcontrol center on wheels - would be used at the Acton site to discharge the marines on the ground floor prior to their disastrous procession into the AP station's lower levels. Months earlier, Kon Cobb had taken a crack at designing it. \"Originally the APC was going to be built from scratch, so I came up with a wedge-shaped design that had kind of a salamander look to it. What I wanted to do most with it was give it rear wheels that could turn a full 360 degrees so the vehicle could rotate on its own wheel base. I'm sure there would have been major problems trying to actually build something like that for real, but it was an interesting idea.\" Though well-received, Cobb's design ultimately fell victim to budgetary constraints. The cost of building such a vehicle from the ground up was simply prohibitive. In fact, building any vehicle from the ground up was no longer a viable option. A more economical approach would have to be found. A solution came with the discovery that the ground crew at Heathrow Airport in London was replacing two of its tractors used for towing jet aircraft. For a reasonable price they would let one of them go. \"We did quite a few alterations on it,\" said Peter Lamont. \"When we got it the vehicle weighed seventy-two tons. The outer structure was four-inch plate steel and the wheel arches were lined with lead - which I guess is necessary because of the sheer weight required to pull those jumbo jets around. So the first thing we did was strip off almost all of the outer plate. That cut it down to about twenty-eight tons. Then we put our own quarter-inch steel plate over that - actually a firm outside the studio did it. Jim came up with a basic concept for how it should be modified and Tony Rimmington, one of my nuts-and-bolts draftsmen, came up with a model. The problem was in getting the work done in only three weeks, especially"],["21","Aliens","CINEFEX 27 • 21"],["22","Aliens","22 - CINEFEX 27"],["23","Aliens","since we were having a problem at the studio because of the volume of work in the metal shop and the fact that another film called Gunbus was already in production and thus had priority. Eventually we found a company outside the studio that works in ferrous and nonferrous building materials and we took it over to there - which was a bit of a chore, but they managed to complete it in about two-and-a-half weeks. When we got it back, it was exactly what we wanted. Then the studio-built components were added on - lights and things like that - and it was painted. It was quite an amazing vehicle. Jim got it up to about thirty-five miles an hour one day and the thing could really swing around. It had four-wheel drive, four-wheel steering and a crab device - all of which we wanted. And during the shooting we were fortunate to have one of the drivers from London Airport who could handle this thing like a dream.\" Though the ground level floor of the power plant had been reinforced to accept the heavy load, there was still concern about its structural integrity. As a result, the APC's use within the facility was held to a minimum. To obtain the balance of the footage required, a sixth-scale replica was constructed by the model shop. \"Because we had to build so much so fast,\" said McClung, \"the original APC - which was needed for some of the first shots - was put together very quickly. Nigel Brackley built the basic structure out of existing radio-control car parts. It had a full suspension system, four-wheel drive and four-wheel steering so it could crab around. It was pretty neat. The body we built entirely out of ABS plastic, a heavy styrene-type material we thought would be tough enough to withstand all the knocking about the APC would have to take - crashing into walls and things like that. The problem was it was just too heavy. Basically, the rear wheels wouldn't turn because of the weight. Eventually we did get quite a few shots with it, but it could only make slight turns and it had no speed whatsoever. So we went back and made a second one out of Kevlar - which is a carbon fiber type material that's very light but very strong. It's used on boats and airplanes and it's virtually bulletproof. Once that body was transferred, the APC was much lighter and could do more. In the back it had a mechanized gun turret that was stowed away when the APC was in the drop-ship, but then when it was on the ground it would roll up the back, stop and rotate forward. That was something they couldn't have done on the full-size APC because it would have weighed a ton. Instead of the usual fiberglass tires, we made ours out of urethane rubber just so they'd be a little bit spongy and react more like real tires would. Whether it's anything you can see I really don't know, but we made the attempt. With the practical lighting we were anticipating that matching the spotlights to the big one would be difficult and that we would also have trouble shooting at high speeds - mainly because a light that looks bright at 24 frames per second just doesn't look bright any more at 48 frames per second. Fortunately, that turned out not to be a problem. There was a lighting expert at the studio named Tom Lee who was able to find a lot of high-powered reflector lamps for us, and those worked out very nicely.\" The miniature armored personnel carrier would get its principal workout during the sequence in which Ripley commandeers the vehicle and goes tearing through the AP station to rescue the besieged troopers trapped in the lower sub-levels. Preliminary shots on the ground level were photographed at the Acton location using the full-size vehicle. Subsequent scenes of it careening down the access ramps, however, were relegated to the model unit. \"Jim wanted the APC to slide around corners and smash into the ramp walls going very fast,\" said McClung, \"and there was no way the full-size vehicle could do that. For one thing, it could only go about thirty miles an hour and if they'd tried to undercrank it, it would have looked phony. With our model we were actually able to overcrank it a bit. First we had to build a miniature to match part of the Acton interior - Bob Skotak and Peter Russell did the design. It had to be pretty good size because the vehicle alone was about four feet long. The footage was shot very well - from low angles and with tracking moves - and we used a special mechanism that would throw out a big shower of sparks as it scraped against the walls.\" By the time cast and crew returned from Acton - joined now by Sigourney Weaver - the effects unit was definitely under the gun. Among the earliest scenes on the schedule were several key process shots that followed sequentially the troopers' demoralizing rout in the AP station. Thanks to Ripley's heroics, those fortunate enough to have survived are free and at least temporarily safe, but their vehicle has been disabled. Stranded midway between the station and the colony complex, they radio back for pickup. The drop-ship flight crew responds promptly, unaware that an alien warrior has slipped on board their craft. Moments after liftoff - to the horror of those awaiting rescue - the dropship careens wildly out of control, crashes into a rock formation and tumbles end over end into the atmosphere processing station. \"Like most everything else in the film,\" said Ron Cobb, \"the drop-ship went through a lot of hands. Syd Mead did a version of it, I did several and so did Jim. All of them were based on helicopter concepts. Mine was something like a Huey with large outriggers on the sides. Cameron's was much more conventional, but in a way a better design - it wasn't quite as weird as mine. We went back and forth on it for quite awhile - which was difficult for me. Usually I have a good grasp on whatever I'm designing because I wholly engineer it myself and think everything through. On this one, though, I was a little bit lost from time to time. When you're working on someone else's concepts, you just have to try and get close to what you think that person means - which was something I wasn't accustomed to doing. As a result, there were lots of things Jim wanted that I had trouble seeing. Not that there was anything wrong with them - I just saw them differently. Jim's an astounding artist with a very strong visual sense, and if he'd had the time he certainly could have designed the whole picture himself.\" Colonial marines storm the colony complex. A tractor used for towing jet aircraft - stripped of excess ballast, but still weighing twenty-eight tons - was redressed with military accoutrements and employed as the troopers' armored personnel carrier. / Since the full-size vehicle was limited in speed and maneuverability, a sixthscale miniature was also constructed. Effects technicians prepare the radio-controlled stand-in for a scene in which it was to sail over a bump after ramming its way out of the AP station. The ramp - not visible in the shot - was used to generate additional speed and momentum. / Though Stan Winston's principal responsibility on the film was creating the alien lifeforms, he and his crew also prepared a lightweight replica of actress Carrie Henn that Sigourney Weaver was able to carry without difficulty during a climactic chase through the station. CINEFEX 27 • 23"],["24","Aliens","The first of Stan Winston's screen was the alien facehugger which - unlike its predecessor in Alien - had the ability to run about and climb with its eight spindly examines one of the rigored dissection scene. / One of huggers - still alive - had to be equipped with a cableactuated tail and tongue that lifeforms found preserved creatures to appear on fingers. Tom Woodruff creatures constructed for use in the med lab statis tubes. / Shane Mahan dresses another for a the statis tube faceunderwater. / Gorman (William Hope), Bishop (Lance Henriksen), Burke (Paul Reiser) and Hicks (Michael Biehn) study remains of the lethal in the med lab. / Later, Bishop dissects one to determine what can be could be operated learned about it. 24 4 CINEFEX 27 \"The drop-ship was something that I could see very clearly \"but for some reason I couldn't in my mind, \" Cameron explained,' seem to draw it. It was like being tongue-tied when you're a public speaker. It just defied me. The main problem was not the overall configuration, but the variable geometry concept of having the gun pods fold out and lock into place. Finally what I did was drive in to the studio one weekend and build a model of it. Then I gave it to Ron Cobb and had him do what he calls 'package it. The finished design was pretty much what resulted.\" Decidedly nonaerodynamic in appearance, with retractable landing skids, the ramp-bottomed drop-ship was heavily armored and capable ofairlifting a combat-equipped APC and discharging it from a near-hover. In its fully deployed configuration, the craft suggested a mechanoid insect bristling with high-tech firepower. With armature retracted, however, it more appropriately resembled a helicopter gunship without rotors. \"Specifically it looks like an Apache AH-64 helicopter,\" said Cameron, \"which isn't too surprising since that's what I started from. What I wanted was to suggest an existing piece of military hardware and then extrapolate beyond that and make it a transatmospheric vehicle. Obviously it wouldn't have rotors, but it does have VTOL (vertical takeoff and landing) and vectored thrust and things like that. Even the motion of it is very helicopter-like. The nose drops down when it accelerates forward and the ship sort of spins in place as it descends. All of that was very intentionally imitated from helicopter movements.\" Once its basic structure and cosmetic details were settled upon, the approved drop-ship designs were rushed to the model shop. \"In a sense, there wasn't a main supervisor for the model shop,\" said Pat McClung, \"Since there was so much to do, most of the jobs were just divided up. The drop-ship, for example, was overseen by Brendan Alimo, one of the guys from the art department. The basic model was about six feet long - one-twelfth scale - and there must have been eight or ten of them built. The pattern for it was made by Peter Astin, an outside contractor who also produced the fiberglass shells that were then brought over to the model shop for detailing. Only one of them was fully mechanized with retractable landing gear and unfolding weapons pods and a door on the bottom for the APC to come out. The rest were built in the fully-deployed position. Some of them - like the one which is lowered into the pressure lock inside the miniature cargo hold - were very carefully detailed. Others only had to crash and blow up, so they were constructed a little more crudely.\" It was the crash-and-blow-up' models that would be needed for the early process plates. \"Because this was a Jim Cameron film,\" said Bob Skotak, \"the directions were very specific. It wasn't just a crash. The ship had to come veering in at a certain angle, hit the top of a rock, shear off the skids, hit the ground at a specified point and then tumble over the camera, exploding and sending off pieces. To do that, we had to break the action into two pieces. The first piece was done on a wire rig similar to the kind the Lydeckers used to use - two parallel lines"],["25","Aliens","CINEFEX 27 • 25"],["26","Aliens","26 • CINEFEX 27"],["27","Aliens","through the ship to support and direct it, and then a third one to pull it along. With that rig, the ship came in and struck the rock, began to angle down and eventually hit the ground. We had three cameras shooting at 120 frames per second, and we did it several times to get it just right. For the second part of the shot, the ship was set up low to the ground and a tensioned tug wire yanked it through the set toward the plate camera. When it hit and started to tumble, a series of explosions were triggered sequentially.\" John Richardson, who on previous assignments had been accustomed to overseeing the model unit as well, had more than enough to occupy himself in other areas. He did, however, supervise most of the miniature pyrotechnics. \"When the dropship hit the ground,\" Richardson explained, \"a small explosion had to go off inside. At that point, the ship had to roll once, explode again and then keep exploding as it tumbled along the ground and over the camera - all at what would appear to be about two hundred miles per hour. As with all miniature explosions - most of which are multiple explosions that come together to form a big one - the problem was one of timing. To get the effect we wanted, I rigged four or five explosions that in real time should have gone off at maybe one-second intervals. But if you hit them at one-second intervals when you're shooting high-speed - say 120 frames per second - you're going to have five-second intervals and an explosion that lasts about half a minute. That's not usually appropriate, so you have to work out exactly what time lag you want between each explosion and then compensate for the shutter speed. Trying to time explosions to fifths of a second is no easy task, but I have several little trick ways of doing it including running the whole thing off one wire and one switch so it does its own timing. Another thing - when you have four or five explosions going off inside a model, you have to be careful that the first explosion doesn't damage the wires for all the rest. Otherwise you'll get one little explosion and then nothing else. So it's quite difficult and involved - and if you've got the thing flying down a wire at fifty miles an hour and hitting a rock and blowing up, it all gets progressively more complicated and confusing.\" With as many elements as there were - and virtually none of them precisely repeatable - three full-blown takes were required to successfully capture the dropship crash on film. Though most of the process work would be rear projection, Cameron opted for front projection on the crash shot. 'With front projection,\" Bob Skotak continued, \"you have a background that is for all practical purposes at infinity. You can do a push-in or a pull-back, but the size of the projected image stays the same to the camera - only the foreground changes. What Jim wanted to do was put his characters in front of the screen and then do a pull-back as they run towards the camera with the ship tumbling in after them. He did a little test with it that way on the stage and I thought it looked quite good, but what he finally wound up doing was a pan-off. The ship comes in, the people run off to the side where there's a rock that conceals the edge of the screen, and then beyond that is a scenic backdrop that continues the same look that was on the process plate. When we did the shot, there were two other cameras off to the sides just to get cutting pieces - but as a result, the set had to be dressed and lit to work from three different angles, which was difficult. We were really pushing the limit of what can be done, but the sequence cuts together very fast and seems to work well.\" Successive shots in the sequence - several quick cuts of the drop-ship tumbling into the AP station and exploding once again - were considerably less pressing and thus postponed until later in the schedule. At that point, the twelve-foot cutout of the station - painted by studio veteran Ernie Smith for use at the far end of the colony street set - was erected in front of a scenic backdrop and dressed with an array of threedimensional props including one of the giant radiating fins Cameron had added to the overall concept for scale. \"For the maw area,\" explained Bob Skotak, \"we cut a hole in the artwork - about three feet wide by eight feet long - and quickly built an interior out of plastic pipes and leftover bits and pieces from other miniatures that we'd done. We also took small-scale rocks we'd been using for some of our shots and put them around outside. For the drop-ship, all we had was a foot-long hunk of plastic that was roughly the right shape. We took that, placed it on a wire with tension and then yanked it into the station. Brian Smithies, who was operating the pyro, had the toughest job - his timing had to be right on. Other than that, it was pretty straightforward. The one element that made it a little bit unusual was the fact that because the ship was so small it had to tumble across only a foot or so away from the camera. Since the ship was already on fire, it wasn't safe to put anybody in there. As a result, we had to get the camera up to 120 frames per second, run out, pull the ship through, trigger the explosions and then run back in and shut the camera down. We used a lot of film that way.\" Despite success with the painted cutout, it was the fullyrendered six-foot miniature that would be used for most of the AP station shots - including a few that were done even before it was finished. \"We were playing catch-up from day one,\" said Bob Skotak. \"One of the first things we had to do was come up with a process plate for a shot in the operations room at the colony. The drop-ship has crashed into the AP station and set off a chain reaction. The temperature inside is rising, and as the characters look out the window they see the station venting - which tells them they have only four hours to get off the planet. The plate itself was quite difficult. It had to be done in-camera; and in order to get the venting effect, we had to shoot the sixfoot station straight away and then use a beam-splitter to reflect in a burst of steam. We tried various-sized plumes of steam and freon - from three feet to maybe fifteen feet - and various camera speeds. What we finally used was steam - about a twelvefoot plume, overcranked. The steam had to be backlit hot and the miniature had to be lit with precisely cued reactive lighting - which was no easy task since with the steam we never Acheron's atmosphere processing station was a thousand-foot-tall structure represented by a six-foot miniature. / For a high-angle view of the armored personnel carrier driving into the facility, a photographic enlargement of the station was reworked into a matte painting. With a beam-splitter, the tiny vehicle was then reflected into the two-dimensional setting. Bob Skotak adds a few last-minute brush strokes prior to photography. / Using a video camera clamped to a makeshift boom arm, Skotak prepares for a point-of-view approach shot that would later be featured on monitors inside the APC. CINEFEX 27 • 27"],["28","Aliens","Inside the AP station, smartDrake (Mark Rolston) scan the area for signs of hostile employed in the film were firearms by John Richardson powered smart-guns were reconfigured Spandau MG-42 machine guns mounted on modification were selected largely on the basis of highintensity muzzle flash and minimal smoke. Vasquez opens fire as alien warriors descend on the earth troops. gun operators Vasquez (Jenette Goldstein) and lifeforms. At Cameron's insistence, weapons adapted from existing and his crew. The highSteadicam harnesses. / Weapons chosen for 28 • CINEFEX 27 knew exactly when it was going to come up. The shot was complicated even further by the fact that it required multiple passes. There was the basic beauty pass first, then the steam pass with the reactive lighting, and then a third pass at about six frames per second to get the practical lighting up to the brightness we needed. We got it done in time, but it was just by the hair of our chinny-chin-chins.\" Even at that, the model was not exactly finished. \"The basic structure was complete, but in order to make our deadline we had to get this shot before all the detailing was done. Right up to the last minute we were trying to dress it up. There'd be a three-minute delay while the steam was being aligned and I'd run over with colored chalk and draw some detail on the side of the model, or Steve Begg would dash in with a can of spray paint or lay down a few quick strips of styrene - whatever we could do to create the impression of detail. That's the thing I remember most about that period - trying to get shots out of models that weren't ready to shoot, but shooting them anyway.\" As the main unit moved into the colony interiors - specifically the control room and the adjoining med lab sets - Stan Winston and his crew braced themselves for their next major assignment. Not only would there be another all-out battle with the alien warriors, but also a smaller-scale confrontation with the creature in its most visceral form. The face-hugger - one of H.R. Giger's nastier conceptions - had had little to do in the first film. In fact, after its initial leap from the egg, it did nothing at all but cling repulsively to Kane's face while leisurely completing its life-cycle function of implanting an alien embryo down its host victim's throat. Without compromising the basic concept, Cameron's script gave the face-hugger a much more active role - even a major scene. While Ripley naps in the med lab with Newt - a plucky nineyear-old whose resourcefulness has rendered her the only human survivor of the Acheron colony - the ever-scheming and treacherous Burke locks them in after setting free two of the predator parasites. Without weapons, the intended victims must stave off the alien attackers which now have the capability of running across floors, climbing over furniture and flinging themselves through the air. In physical appearance the face-huggers remained essentially the same - slimy face-size bodies with eight strong but spindly fingers, a pair of scrotum-like sacs and a long tail. \"We took a few artistic liberties,\" said Stan Winston, \"nothing anyone's even likely to notice. It was just one of those things. If you work on something long enough, you're bound to find things you feel can be a little bit improved. Sculpturally, I think ours was a little more organic than the first one, although the first one was brilliant. The finger appendages on ours are a little more like fingers than they were on the original. We made the knuckles a little more knuckle-like and on the tips of the fingers we actually put nails. Basically we took that which we saw as the intent of the original design and carried it a step further. Also, we lengthened the tail by about six inches so we could do more work with it - wrapping it around necks and getting a whiplike action out of it.\" As with most of the other alien forms, preliminary work on the face-hugger began before Winston and his core crew departed for London. With the original creature on hand as a study guide, sculptor Alec Gillis rendered the new and somewhat revised version that would be featured in the sequel. Concurrently, Lance Anderson got a start on some of the internal mechanisms. While the face-hugger built for the first film was positionable but nonarticulated, the ones created for Aliens required a full range of motion. In fact, to accomplish all the actions called for in the script, a number of different variants were devised. When first seen in the med lab, a full half-dozen are on display - floating, and apparently lifeless, in rows of statis tubes. As Carter Burke steps in for a closer look, however, one of the creatures lunges at his face - tail thrashing and underbelly exposed to reveal a tonguelike insemination organ trying to penetrate the glass. \"For the statis tubes,\" said Winston, \"we had to develop the look of dead, rigored face-huggers with flaking skin - and, of course, the two that were still alive. Those are the ones that get loose and go after Ripley. For the one that slams up against the inside of the tube, the difficulty was that it had to be operated underwater. The tube had to have a water-tight seal, but we had to be able to move in and out with our cables. Getting the tail to whip around in a confined space and underwater was a major challenge. We tried various things such as air pressure and water pressure, but nothing seemed to work. Finally, Ray Lovell came up with a spring-loaded tail that was cable-operated. Pulling on the cable would curl the tail up very tightly and then releasing it would allow it to whip open. At the same time, two other cables moved the base of the tail in a 360-degree axis so that it would pivot around while the tail itself was whipping. It worked beautifully.\" The primary face-hugger - which, among other things, lashes its tail around Ripley's neck and tries to force itself upon her - was a fully-articulated, cable-controlled puppet begun in the United States by Lance Anderson and completed in England by Steve Norrington and others. \"Lance worked out most of the finger mechanisms before we left for England. Then, after we got over there, the tail whipping action and the extruding tongue elements were added, along with some fine-tuning of the controls. Everything worked on that one, requiring something like nine operators. For scenes when it's crawling up the table, we built another one that was basically the same except that it didn't have the tongue element within the body. Then lan Rolph worked on a third one that had just the finger articulations. That was to help it scurry along and turn around on the wall before leaping off. We also made a series of floppy face-huggers that had articulated fingers, but the fingers were left loose so the creatures could be thrown around like you'd throw a dummy off a cliff. Some of those were also used in the scenes where the facehuggers are blown up. It's amazing how some really good dynam-"],["29","Aliens","ics came out of stuff as simple as that. We'd be wondering what sort of fancy doodad to come up with for a particular shot and Jim would say, 'Let's just make up a bunch of dummies - we'll throw them and blow them up.' And for quick cuts that worked.\" Also begun in the United States was the time-consuming task of devising and constructing a face-hugger that could scuttle convincingly across the floor. \"I wanted to do a pull-toy type of thing,\" said Winston, \"where we would literally pull it across the floor and a wheel would turn underneath or something and cause the legs to move. In a way, that's pretty much what we ended up doing; but at the start we couldn't quite figure out how to do it so we got off on a few tangents. One of the ideas Jim had was to use either a high-torque electric motor or a little model airplane gasoline engine. I have so much respect for him that I'll try anything he comes up with, but we were getting nowhere with that idea. Finally Jim called me from England and said: 'I don't know why I didn't think of this, Stan, but I did this thing for Piranha II where a fish was pulled through the water over a wire and we had a little mechanism inside to make the tail wiggle.' So, working from that idea, he drew out a little design of what he thought the insides could be for the facehugger, sent it over here, and Rick Lazzarini proceeded to make it work - which was quite a job since there were still an awful lot of problems to overcome. The face-hugger was on a wire that wrapped around a rubber-surfaced gear on the inside and was held taut by two operators on either end. Pulling the face-hugger along that wire would cause the gear to move which in turn would move other smaller gears connected to the legs. The gearing mechanism turned out to be very intricate, but the simple brilliance of the idea was that there was a correlation between how fast you pulled the face-hugger and how fast the legs would go. If you pulled it fast, the legs would move fast; if you pulled it slow, the legs would go slow.\" Though process plates would continue to be needed throughout the production, once the most pressing ones were completed the effects unit could begin directing its attention into other areas. By now the sixth-scale colony miniature - extending from one corner of M stage to the other - was ready to shoot. Ten shots, in all, were planned - most earmarked for the illfated opening sequence designed to establish the colony and its workaday routine. Low 'eye-level' camera angles were used exclusively to impart the sense that a real environment - not a miniature - was being photographed. Dust and debris were blown about with giant wind machines and radio-controlled vehicles were constructed, driven through shots and then redressed to look different for subsequent scenes. To further suggest that this was a real environment inhabited by real people, two shots were devised to incorporate live performers. One was a fairly traditional perspective shot. As the camera followed a sixth-scale vehicle down the street, it would pan across the miniature buildings behind - two of which were separated by a distance of several feet. On the opposite end of CINEFEX 27 • 29"],["30","Aliens","30 • CINEFEX 27"],["31","Aliens","the stage - visible through the opening - a full-scale vehicle was positioned against an appropriate backing. By carefully aligning the camera and pumping enough light into the stage to maintain full depth-of-field, the distant vehicle could be made to look as though it were on the same plane as the miniature. Thus, as the camera followed the radio-controlled vehicle down the sixth-scale street, in the alleyway behind - or at least apparently so - two colonists could be seen covering their full-size vehicle with a tarp. The second shot - also achieved in-camera - was somewhat more complex. A scene was desired showing a couple of people entering the boomtown's well-trafficked saloon. \"Since the only set we had was a miniature,\" said Dennis Skotak, \"what we did was build a small section representing the doorway leading into the bar. We came up with a massive safe door that was actually a stock unit from another film and we dressed it up to give it our characteristic Acheron 'bad-side-of-town' look. We even put a Stroh's neon sign on the wall. Jim was very much into that sort of thing. He felt that in order to relate to the future, there needed to be a sense of immediacy about it. That's why a lot of what we did - even the vehicles and the tractors - are just extrapolations of what we have now. Anyway, once we finished our full-size doorway area, we positioned it at 90 degrees to the colony set. The camera faced the full-size set, shooting through a large sheet of glass placed at a 45-degree angle to the camera. For the area we wanted to extend with the miniature, we backed the glass with black paper. Then we reflected the image of the miniature colony complex into the glass, balancing the lighting so that the two areas matched. Basically, it was an in-camera split-screen that allowed us to line everything up and monitor the shot right through the camera rather than shoot two separate pieces and put them together in an optical printer.\" The bustling complex would have a different appearance later in the film when the colonial marines arrive in their armored personnel carrier. Aside from a few abandoned vehicles, the streets would now be empty. \"When the troopers first land on the planet, it's raining,\" said Pat McClung, \"which created quite a mess. The stage floor was covered with plastic, and on top of that was a layer of ash that had to be wetted down. I'm not sure exactly what it was, but it was really dirty stuff and everybody complained about it for months. We needed it though because Jim wanted our miniature APC to kick up all kinds of mud and splatters as it drove by. The set looked pretty good. We had things like doors slamming in the breeze and little lamps flopping back and forth - typical ghost town stuff - and we used little silicone sleevings for cables that were slung around and dangling in the breeze. Then we had pressurized insect sprayers filled with water that sprayed a fine mist in front of the camera so it would look like a light rainfall. It gave everything a nice sense of scale. And of course the entire stage was fogged up to give the feel of aerial perspective.\" Despite all the effort expended on it, only one panoramic shot of the colony would make its way into the final For the interior of the station, Peter Lamont's art department prepared biomechanical shapes from fiberglass and vacuformed plastic which then were fitted over existing structures in the deactivated power plant. / Members of the colonial assault team edge their way into the murky depths of the station. The area directly over their heads is a hanging miniature used to expand the apparent size of the alien encrustation. / Effects cameraman Harry Oaks and camera operator David Litchfield take measurements during construction. / In the cocoon chamber, one of the colonists is discovered alive but with an alien embryo growing inside her. As the troopers watch in horror, the savage parasite rips itself free. To achieve the effect, actress Barbara Coles was positioned on a slant board so that her own head and arms were joined to a foam rubber torso. Two articulated chest-bursters were then employed - one for the actual burst and a second, more articulated version to flail about afterwards. / The inner mechanism for the second chest-burster as constructed by Stephen Norrington. CINEFEX 27 • 31"],["32","Aliens","Included as set dressing in the AP station were sculpted casts contorted in pain - the sole remains of most of alive to serve as hosts for Unaware that a warrior is perfectly camouflaged in the encrusted with the resinous alien excretion. / Without warning, the alien swoops trooper away before she or any of her compatriots can do anything to prevent it. figures topped with lifethe colonists who were carried off and cocooned the alien parasites. / lurking just above her, biomechanoid structure, Dietrich (Cynthia Scott) passes in front of a wall down and whisks the 32 • CINEFEX 27 The film's other significant casualty - the Jordens' discovery ofthe derelict spacecraft - was also a major effects undertaking. Preliminary scenes were to show the family vehicle lurching its way over the sector's uncharted terrain. \"I did an initial design on the little explorer truck that the family was on when they find the ship,\" said Ron Cobb. \"Right away I wanted to make it a Winnebago so you'd figure it was a family outing. That didn't survive, though, because Jim got excited about something else based on another design. But I came up with this strange little eight-wheeled thing, with a boat on the back and a dirt bike, bumping slowly through the planet.\" The approved design - a spidery all-terrain vehicle - was conceived by Cameron, developed by art director Fred Hole and then turned over to the model shop for construction. Consistent with the other ground vehicles, it would be built in one-sixth scale. \"It was an eight-wheel design,\" explained Pat McClung, \"with a very strange cantilevered suspension system that had kind of a scissor action to it. It was a crazy-looking thing and it proved very difficult to build, especially since the designs had to be produced exactly to match a full-size version that was going Bore made or o to be made. For our miniature, we got a guy named Nigel Brackley, who was recommended by John Richardson, to do the radio control and mechanical work and he turned out to be very good. Between him and Jamie Thomas, who built the body and the arms for suspension, the tractor came out really well.\" In the end, however, its full-scale counterpart would be somewhat truncated. \"Our original intent had been to build the whole tractor full-size,\" said Peter Lamont. \"That would have allowed us to shoot whatever we wanted with it - exteriors and interiors. In addition to the opening scenes, we also planned to use it later in the film. Originally, the marines were supposed to drive up to the colony and find the Jorden tractor jammed into the main entrance as though the colonists had tried to keep the aliens out by barricading the way. Ultimately, though, it became expendable. When funds started getting tight, one of the things we decided we could get by with was building only part of the tractor and then shooting it tight or partially concealed behind pieces of landscape. So all we built was the front section. Only three of the eight wheels were visible and for those we took castings from real tires used on a crane and widened them out. Then, to keep everything very light, the vehicle itself was carved from polystyrene and mounted on plywood.\" Since the full-scale tractor was incapable of movement - independent or otherwise - all the traveling shots were achieved with the radio-controlled miniature. As a result, an important adjunct to the model-building effort was the construction of appropriately-scaled landscape settings. \"For those,\" explained Bob Skotak, \"Jim had done some drawings and I had also done a few. What we tried to create was a slightly more elaborate version of what was done in the first film. Those were sort of Gigeresque rocks. Well, these were more like Cameronesque rocks - similar to the others, but a bit more stylized. Most of the hands-on work was done by Chris Overs, Mick Milford and the Pinewood plaster shop. The rocks were first carved out of styrofoam, then covered with plaster and a layer of wax. On top of that would be another layer of plaster and another layer of wax and so on. After several layers had been built up, we could hammer away at different sections and it would shear off in kind of a shale-like, sheeting effect. Using that technique, we created about thirty or forty rocks that we were then able to assemble and reassemble in different ways to create our miniature landscapes - most of which incorporated perspective effects, forcing distance by using rocks of diminishing size leading away from the camera.\" By a curious happenstance, the Aliens effects unit found it unnecessary to build another derelict spacecraft from scratch. The original structure - nearly twelve feet in diameter - had been given by Fox to science fiction collector Bob Burns for his promotional efforts on behalf of the first film. After nearly eight years, though, it was in less than pristine condition. Sections of the carved styrofoam structure had crumbled off and the plasticene coating of surface detail had largely been rubbed away. At one point the derelict derelict had even come close to being sent to the dump. A last-minute reprieve had saved it, however, and it wound up in storage - at the L.A. Effects Group facility in North Hollywood. \"We needed several shots of the derelict ship for process plates,\" said Bob Skotak, \"but because of its weight and the condition it was in, it made more sense to shoot it in Los Angeles than to try and ship it over to England. So while the project was still in development - before everybody moved over to Pinewood - we had to refurbish the derelict, build a landscape for it and shoot several shots at the L.A. Effects facility. So that was the beginning. Our first day of real work on the film was vacuRequired initially were point-of-view tracking shots approaching the ship that could later be used as process plates for scenes within the Jorden tractor. \"The scenes we did were all throughthe-window shots,\" Skotak continued. \"We made some approximate calculations on the size of the derelict and figured out that it had been built at about one-ninetieth scale. Naturally then, our landscape - which was covered with lava and pumicelike rock - had to be similarly scaled. When it was finished, we smoked up the set and ran our camera along this tiny little road, ned down hit and up and down hills and around various outcroppings, until we came right up to the derelict. In one version, we ran right up to the crack in the side where the Jordens were going to enter the ship. At that close a range, however, the model didn't hold up well. So we decided to cut the scene in the middle and when we got to England we built a much-enlarged, one-fifth scale section and continued the move on it. Interestingly, the two shots were used within a few seconds of each other - one done in Los Angeles and the other done months later by an entirely different crew in England - and they cut together perfectly.\" Perspective shots and beam-splitter photography - which had already been used in the colony and would become standard"],["33","Aliens","CINEFEX 27 • 33"],["34","Aliens","34 - CINEFEX 27"],["35","Aliens","in-camera compositing techniques for the film - were also employed in the derelict sequence. To obtain a shot of the tractor pulling up to the base of the monolithic craft, a tabletop landscape setting was quickly assembled in front of a black backdrop with the camera shooting up from apparent ground level. Between the camera and the set was a beam-splitter which reflected into the black backdrop area a large side-mounted painting of the derelict rendered by Peter Melrose and refined by Bob Skotak. By careful alignment through the camera, Skotak was able to mask the lower portion of the painting in black to correspond with the miniature landscape and tractor and thus produce a seamless in-camera matte. A later shot of the Jordens standing outside the derelict was achieved with the enlarged fifth-scale mockup used earlier in the plate photography. By placing the actors well to the rear of the model and carefully aligning them through a crack in the hull, large amounts of light and a deep-focus lens made them appear to be standing in the foreground looking up at the ship. \"To justify for the die-hard fans why the acoustic beacon from the first film was not picked up by the colonists and why they were therefore unaware of the existence of the derelict,\" Cameron noted, \"we had the ship smashed sideways into a rock and partially broken up by a recent outflow of lava. And when the Jorden family - the mother and father - go inside, they don't go in through the portals that the original party used. To give things a somewhat different look, even in that one area where I was following directly in the footsteps of the other film, I had them go in through a large rent in the hull. Inside, one of my first ideas was to do a scene where they actually find the egg chamber and all the eggs come to life. But it would have cost us a million dollars just to create the same basic imagery we'd already been shown in Alien, so I decided to treat that whole aspect as inferred. We know what's inside the ship - we don't need to see it again.\" By extension, the same philosophy applied to the sequence as a whole. When Cameron's tightly-edited final assembly checked in at slightly more than two-and-a-half hours, he and Hurd agreed to cut it back by fifteen minutes. In the process, both the colony prologue and derelict rediscovery were jettisoned. Elsewhere on the lot, Peter Lamont and his crew were still scrambling to keep ahead of the first unit. Working from his own concepts for the Gateway station, Ron Cobb's for the colony and Syd Mead's for the Sulaco- each filtered through the critical eye of Jim Cameron - the veteran production designer had more than an adequate amount of outside input. \"I've been around long enough to appreciate all the help I can get,\" said Lamont. \"What matters eventually is what you see on the screen and not who started the inkling of ideas - things spring up and change anyway. On this picture it was like a huge jigsaw, just trying to get the very best from everyone's input. Especially challenging was the Sulaco's cargo hold which, when constructed, would crowd the walls and ceiling of one of Pinewood's Exercising artistic license, Cameron and Winston effected various subtle changes in the alien's physiognomy. The most radical departure, however, was not apparent on film. While the original alien suit had been made from rubber, the suits employed for the sequel were simple leotards covered with foam rubber pieces, thus affording much greater freedom of movement. By carefully framing his shots, choreographing insect-like movements and utilizing various camera tricks, Cameron succeeded in disguising the fact that his warriors were essentially men in suits. / Whenever possible, puppets were employed as they were considerably thinner than the live performers and could be positioned in ways no human could. / In addition, a cable-operated head and torso assembly was constructed for inserts. / John Rosengrant and Tom Woodruff work at painting three of the warrior heads. Hanging behind them are two of the finished suits. / As the marines beat a hasty retreat, one of the warriors tries to pry its way into the armored personnel carrier. CINEFEX 27 • 35"],["36","Aliens","A six-foot drop-ship sails across the effects stage on a rig consisting of two parallel lines running through the ship - to support and direct it - and a third to pull it along. This rig, a departure from the overhead crane device used predominantly in the film, was needed specifically for the crash scenes where higher-velocity wire work was mandated. During an actual take, the set - backed by a painted sky - would be fogged in with smoke. / As the dropship lifts off to rescue the troopers stranded near the AP station, an alien warrior that has crept on board attacks the pilot (Colette Hiller), sending the craft reeling out of control. / With one weapons pod sheared away, the crippled drop-ship rests on its wire rig as effects technicians prepare for the final crash and explosion. 36 • CINEFEX 27 largest stages. \"Our main problem was size. Basically, it was a matter of putting a pint pot into a half-pint jug. We wanted a huge set, but there had to be practicalities about it. For one thing, we needed a stage with a tank - both for the cargo lock and for the scenes where Newt is scrambling around under the floor. This proposed difficulties because the tanks on the various stages at Pinewood are in different places. They're all reasonably central, but on one stage it may be out on one side and on another it may go another way. We spent a whole morning just moving a model around trying to come up with an admirable compromise for how we could fit it all in so it would work. Finally we arrived at a solution where we had an eight-foot pit for the start of the cargo lock and another area where Newt could scramble around under the grills, and yet we still had enough room to maneuver \"What I liked about Aliens, from a design point of view,\" said Syd Mead, \"was the military hardware and the mixture between space and naval architecture. As a commercial designer I've had occasion to create custom interiors for several luxury yachts and aircraft, and so for me this was an interesting opportunity to take the opposite sort of approach in a very similar environment. Anyone who has walked around inside a rigged military helicopter or has seen a 747 fuselage with all its interior panels stripped off is well aware that what you get is an incredible maze of wiring harnesses, hydraulic tubing and high-velocity air conditioning - an electrician's and plumber's nightmare. That was the look I wanted to suggest - no frills and very functional.\" In keeping with this philosophy, Mead's cargo hold designs were conceptually multilayered. The cavernous bay was first rendered as an empty hull - with interior structural supports clearly visible - then modified and dressed with panels and equipment to make it all seem perfectly functional. Since the script called for two drop-ships to be stowed in the cargo hold, Mead's full design - with the shuttles mounted in tandem - could not possibly have been constructed on a single soundstage. The complete set, in fact, would be rendered solely in miniature at a later date. In full scale, only the front third would fit on the largest stage available. Occupying much of that space would be a full-size mockup of the forward drop-ship. \"We were very fortunate,\" explained Lamont, \"to have as one of our outside contractors a company that specialized in aluminum panel beating. Among other things, they do a lot of work on Grand Prix racing cars. At the studio we put up a basic structure made from wood, and then these metal people came over with the aluminum cladding they custom-made for us and pop-riveted the whole thing together. The interior and cockpit were also constructed, as was the ramp which was put into place with huge rams to make it look as if the APC could actually go up it - although in fact it couldn't. To dress the ship, we went through all the scrap yards we could find that dealt in aircraft parts. Although the basic style was pretty much like a helicopter, the legs actually came from a Vulcan bomber - which is just now being phased out of the Royal Air Force - and the skids were made up from flare trays used on the Canberra bomber.\" In addition to the drop-ship, another key artifact in the cargo hold was the power-loader. First introduced as the marines prepare for their descent to the planet surface, the power-loader seemed at best an ingenious but incidental bit of set dressing - a nine-and-a-half foot tall hunk of machinery that Ripley deftly maneuvers as a test of her practical skills in the ship's workaday environment. Not until the final reel of the film would its full dramatic potential be explored. Essentially an anthropomorphized forklift, the power-loader featured an open roll cage in which the operator stood erect. Joystick controls activated a pair of eight-foot-long lifting arms and servo mechanisms allowed for ambulatory movement of its squat hydraulic legs - altogether a powerful piece of equipment that gave the appearance of being grounded in some sort of practical reality. \"I suppose it would be theoretically possible to actually build one,\" said Jim Cameron, \"but I think its value would be negligible. The skill involved in learning how to operate one is so much greater than learning how to operate a wheeled vehicle. The only application might be in a zero gravity environment where a wheeled vehicle would just float off. Something like a powerloader could have magnetic cleats on the feet.\" The on-stage environment at Pinewood was scarcely zero gravity, but a functional power-loader had to be constructed nonetheless. The task fell to John Richardson and his crew. \"Building a power-loader is not the sort of thing you get called on to do every week,\" commented Richardson, \"so it did pose quite a lot of problems - not the least of which was the amount of time we had to do it in. It was late June or early July before we got down to some serious thinking about it, and at that time we were operating under the assumption that we would have to have it ready by October. In the end, we were given until the beginning of November, but even then I was worried that we weren't going to meet our date. Part of the problem was that the power-loader went through an unusually long design phase. A lot of directors would have said, 'I want it to look like a walking forklift,' and let it go at that. Jim would say, 'I want it to look like a walking forklift and those little bolts up there have to be this shape.' He's very pedantic about the fine details and very particular about the overall aesthetic. And, of course, it wasn't just a matter of getting it to look right. It also had to do a lot \"I came up with an original concept for the loader,\" said Cameron, \"but it was really kind of designed while it was being built because there were so many technical limitations and considerations. John Richardson and his people built foam-core mockups of the various sections and then we'd go over them and modify them and see what worked and what didn't. The biggest problem was getting clearances for all the rams in the back so that it could have a full range of movement. It was a very painful process. More painful for him than for me, I'm afraid, because all I had to do was go in and say, 'No, I want it to look like this.' We all hated each other for about a month until we"],["37","Aliens","CINEFEX 27 • 37"],["38","Aliens","38 • CINEFEX 27"],["39","Aliens","realized that the power-loader was actually going to come together and work, and then everybody was enthusiastic again.\" \"Starting with the basic look Jim was after,\" Richardson elaborated, \"we worked out each joint and body part individually. When we came up with something that was basically functional, we would photograph it and measure it and Jim would then take it away and do a pencil drawing of what he wanted it to look like. More often than not the changes were just cosmetic, but then we'd get it back and discover that certain parts didn't work. So we'd rework it and take it back to Jim again. It was a continual backwards and forwards process. Multiply that by about twenty-five times for every joint and part and you begin to get the picture.\" From the beginning it was acknowledged that the powerloader could not be built to actually function as designed. Like most special effects props, a small army of technicians was required to actually put it through its paces. While Sigourney Weaver was the apparent operator, her efforts were augmented considerably by stuntman John Lees who was concealed just inches behind her within the loader's outer shell. Even though the eight-foot arms were counterweighted, wrestling them about would have been difficult for Weaver who, in addition to acting, had other physical business she needed to be doing at the same time. With Lees' weightlifter arms inserted into the loader's, however, Weaver was able to provide direction and steering from the outside while the hidden stuntman provided the actual muscle power from within. The legs were operated in similar \"Every effort was made to make the power-loader as lightweight as possible,\" said Richardson, \"which automatically ruled out most of the materials we normally work in - metal and wood primarily. A lot of other things could be dressed up to look okay, but they would not be strong enough to withstand the kind of knocking about it was going to get. So what we opted to do was build patterns of everything in wood or steel or tetracil - which is a fiberglass car body filler that's easy to mold and shape - and then vacuform them. Even then we had to be careful, however, because a lot of the plastics were too brittle once they'd been formed. We ended up using a straight PVC (polyvinyl chloride) which was very strong and had a slight leather-grain finish that looked as though it had been cast. We doubled up those vacuformed shells and backfilled them with rigid foam to give them strength, and then used aluminum bearings and rods for reinforcement at the pivot points.\" Making the power-loader fully functional would add considerably more weight. \"The operation of the claws proved to be tricky,\" Richardson continued. \"Even though they were built out of fairly lightweight materials, they were still pretty heavy just because they were so large. We knew that the electric motors, or whatever else we used to control the wrist swivel and the claw rotation, would have to be very light because they would be right out at the end of the arm. What we came up with were some very high-powered radio-control servos that we rewired to work on a direct wire route or through a normal radio transmitter. The clasping mechanisms we decided to run manually using bowden cables. We did that because in certain situations they had to pick things up and we were concerned that if they were motorized it might be difficult to control the tension. Basically, we were worried about breaking the claws themselves or whatever it was they were picking up or even the arm of the machine. We decided the best way to do it would be to run cables off and let one of our operators actually 'feel' the tension. That approach worked quite well, and we found that without wires or other assistance the claws could lift about ten or fifteen pounds all by themselves.\" Counterweighting was a major consideration, both from an aesthetic and a functional point of view. Without some means of offsetting the claw-heavy arms, it would have been impossible for even the strongest of internal operators to lift them unaided. So for Richardson, the problem became one of incorporating sufficient weight behind the shoulder pivot points to balance the eight-foot arms without betraying its true function or becoming unsightly in the process. \"Some of the hydraulic rams on the backside of the loader are practical, some are pure dummies and others we were able to use as disguised counterweights. We also kept extending the arms behind the shoulders and weighing them down as much as possible. Part of the reason for that was that Jim kept asking if we could extend the arms another six inches or so, and every time we did it would quadruple the amount of counterweighting we needed on the back.\" By the time it was complete, the power-loader weighed in at nearly six hundred pounds - far more than even a burly stuntman could maneuver with muscle power alone. \"Because of its size and shape and weight,\" said Richardson, \"there was no way it could be free-standing, let alone walk around. So we knew it would have to be supported externally, either from above or below. Hanging it on wires from above turned out to be the most successful because it gave the thing more freedom of movement. We built a special crane arm for it that was mounted on a small electric dolly so we could track forward and backward, and there was also a pivot on the end of the crane jib that enabled the loader to turn. The wires were actually two quarter-inch steel cables that attached to the top. The machine was so big that, more often than not, the support cables were well out of frame; but even when they weren't, we were able to get away with it by making them look like heavy-duty aerials. For low angles we also developed a rigid pole arm that mounted from below.\" Operating the power-loader was a major team effort. \"In addition to the stuntman buried inside, we had somebody driving the electric trolley, about three guys manning the back end of the crane jib for stability, another controlling the pivot at the front end, one or two more operating the swivel and side-toside movement of the wrists, two others opening and closing the claws - and me invariably standing somewhere in the middle screaming my head off at all of them. On occasion, we also had people assisting with the feet. So there was a lot of coorWhen the drop-ship strikes the ground, it bursts into flame and tumbles end over end into the atmosphere processing station. / A rear process plate as seen through the windows of the colony control room. The fire has died down, but explosions have damaged the station's cooling system and emergency venting now signals an imminent nuclear detonation. To achieve the in-camera effect, a twelvefoot burst of steam was overcranked and reflected into the AP station via a beam-splitter. Carefully cued interactive lighting was employed to tie the images together. / Dennis Skotak examines a setup for the shot in which the drop-ship tumbles into the station. In this instance, a twelve-foot painted cutout - used earlier as a backdrop for the colony miniature - was enhanced with threedimensional set pieces. A foot-long drop-ship was then rigged on wires, set ablaze and pulled into the cutaway maw area where miniature pyrotechnics were triggered on impact. CINEFEX 27 • 39"],["40","Aliens","Stan Winston and his crew articulated face-huggers for a sequence in the colony med lab where Ripley and Newt are assailed by a pair of the creatures that have been unleased upon them. Rick Lazzarini examines a mechanism he developed for capable of running across mechanism was built by lan Rolph for a specific shot requiring one of the facehuggers to scurry in a tight circle and then leap off a wall. / Ripley (Sigourey Weaver) struggles with the desperately to keep it from devised a number of wire-pull gearing a face-hugger that was the floor. / Another face-hugger, trying clamping onto her face. 40 • CINEFEX 27 dination involved. We had a few days of rehearsals, which were used primarily to put the thing through its paces and for me to find out what actions worked best and looked best and what actions didn't. It also gave the stuntman inside a chance to get the feel of things. Beyond that we didn't do much in the way of rehearsing specific scenes because with Jim we didn't really know what any given shot was going to entail until we got into shooting it.\" Aside from the power-loader, the cargo hold set would also play host to the alien queen. As conceived by Cameron and executed by Stan Winston, the slavering fourteen-foot matriarch - bent on protecting her young at all cost - would eventually be introduced earlier in the egg chamber, but her first and decidedly most strenuous on-camera workout would be in the Sulaco. \"Right from the start,\" said Winston, \"Jim had a concept of the alien queen in the back of his head. In fact, when we first began talking about the project, he showed me this beautiful rendering he had done of it, which I liked immediately.\" Looking somewhat like a cross between a praying mantis and a tyrannosaurus rex, Cameron's concept was both fresh and visually dramatic, and yet still a clear and logical extrapolation from the basic warrior design originated by H.R. Giger. \"Even though Giger wasn't directly involved,\" said Cameron, \"his ghost sort of hovered about. I must say, though, that I feel a sense of authorship when it comes to the queen. Somebody once described it as an anorexic dinosaur, which I suppose is inevitable even though that's not what I had in mind. In fact, I wanted specifically not to suggest a dinosaur concept - at least overtly - because that would have been a little too commonplace widow spider.\" Cameron's design came complete with a proposal for how it might be rendered as a full-size mechanical artifact. Essentially, the queen would be suspended from an overhead cable rig, with most of its key functions controlled externally - even somewhat routinely - by a combination of rods, wires and hydraulics. Its internal workings, however, would be considerably more unorthodox. To achieve maximum fluidity in the arms - two long, powerful forelimbs at shoulder level and a pair of smaller, secondary ones below - Cameron's plan was to position two stuntmen back-to-back inside the slender torso area. Thus situated, each stuntman could control one large and one small limb with his own extended arms. Though instantly attracted to the basic design, Winston was somewhat hesitant at the prospect of building a large-scale mechanical creature that could function convincingly with two stuntmen inside - not to mention the difficult-to-conceal overhead support mechanism and all the external actuators. Nevertheless, it was an intriguing proposition. \"There were a few little things about the design that I thought could be improved, so I worked up a few sketches of my own and showed them to Jim. Actually they were pretty much like his, although on one of them I had deleted the extra set of arms and reconfigured it so that only a single stuntman would be needed inside. That proposal was rejected. One thing Jim did like, though, was an idea from my redesign of the leg that gave it a double joint and made it look less human. In the end, Jim took into consideration some of what I'd said and the things I'd drawn and he went off and drew another queen which was similar to his first but much more refined. In fact, when he came back, it was obvious that that was the alien queen. There was no doubt about it. We then sat down together and worked out a scale drawing - literally blueprinting her out in profile and front view, with her exact shape and exactly how she would have to be done to get two people inside.\" The next step was to determine if the overall approach was a workable one. \"Once we came up with a design and an idea of how we were going to get it to work, we rented a crane and built a quick little body plate setup out of wood that would hold two stuntmen. Then we made a rough mockup of the queen using black foam-core and plastic trash bags and suspended our stuntmen inside it. For the big arms, we used ski pole extensions - which were lightweight, but very strong - and attached to them some creature hands I had developed for another project. Each stuntman would hold one with his arm stretched out straight so that from his shoulder to his fist would be the queen's upper arm and from his fist to the end of the pole would be her forearm. For the smaller arms in front, the guys were able to use their own arms without any extensions. We set this thing up out in our parking lot to see if it was going to work - and it did. There was still a lot of fine-tuning to be done, but the basic concept was good.\" Still in the United States, Winston put his crew to work on the next phase of the queen construction project - a fully-realized, quarter-scale prototype. \"The miniature queen was designed to fill a variety of needs. First it was a way of actually seeing the thing in three dimensions before we committed to anything larger. Second, it would serve as a maquette for sculpting the full-size queen. And third, the molds we made from it could be used to produce a quarter-scale puppet.\" Although intent upon using Winston's full-scale queen as much as possible, Cameron recognized that certain of the scenes he had written would be impossible to achieve with a fourteenfoot mechanical artifact. Something smaller and more manageable would be required. Two options were readily apparent - a stop-motion puppet or a rod- and cable-actuated one. \"As a director, I find it tough to deal with stop-motion. I was very happy with what was done on The Terminator, but by that point in the story we were dealing with a mechanical device and I didn't feel the look of stop-motion violated anything we'd already done. I was a little more worried about it with Aliens. The scenes involving the alien queen were very important, and what we were trying to do was create a real and believable character. Plus, when we started to analyze the types of shots we'd be doing we"],["41","Aliens","CINEFEX 27 • 41"],["42","Aliens","realized that most of them would require fairly quick action - turns and spins and rapid strides - the sorts of moves that in stop-motion would cause so much displacement per frame that the arms and legs would end up strobing. There are things you just can't do in any other way, though, so originally the plan was to have a rod puppet version and a stop-motion version. But eventually it got down to budget and it became a choice of either one or the other. Given that, the rod-and cable-actuated puppet seemed more appealing for a number of reasons. One was that I had never worked with that kind of thing before and I wanted to fool around with it and see what could be done. Also I just had a feeling that with a lot of the floor effects we'd be using - smoke and steam and that sort of thing - we'd have more flexibility with puppets we could shoot 'live' on a miniaMeanwhile, the queen project was stalled until completion of the miniature prototype. To facilitate the job, Stan Winston assembled a team of sculptors - including most of his permanent crew - and divided up the work. John Rosengrant was given the body: Alec Gillis and Willie Whitten each had a leg; Greg Figiel took the arms; Brian Penikas and Shawn McEnroe worked on the tail; and Shane Mahan sculpted the head. While the miniature queen was coming together in bits and pieces, preliminary work on the full-size version was also underway. \"The first thing we had to do was build the inner body of the queen,\" Winston explained, \"the strong fiberglass shells that would hold the stuntmen and the strong aluminum plate inside that would carry the hydraulics. That inner section would in large part dictate the size of the queen's body, so it was necessary to work that out in advance.\" Rick Lazzarini was in charge of inner body fabrication, assisted by machinist Wayne Sturm. Once completed, it was shipped to London where the bulk of the queen construction would be undertaken. Immediately upon finishing the miniature and generating all necessary molds and castings, Stan Winston and a six-man contingent departed for England. \"We set up shop on a large effects stage at Pinewood and began building the different components of the queen - which basically we designed and built and sculpted exactly as we had done the miniature. Armatures were built for the legs and arms and body and head and tail - all separately. The simplest armature to build was for the tail so I decided we should do that first. Not that it was simple by any stretch of the imagination, but the tail was probably the easiest to build quickly and I figured it would be a good breakin project for my English crew to help me assess individual strengths and weaknesses. Once the tail armature was done, we could then go to clay on it while the rest of the armatures were being built.\" As they would throughout, Ray Lovell and Richard Landon did most of the mechanical work. Directly over their tail armature, the outer segmented sections would then be sculpted in clay by Steve Norrington, John Robertson, Christine Overs and Philomena Davis. A prime requisite for the torso area was that it be large enough 42 A CINFFEY 27 ring pa"],["43","Aliens","to accommodate the stuntmen needed inside to operate the arms. \"Foam human figures representing the two men were placed inside our already-completed body plate,\" said Winston, \"and a wire mesh sculpting armature was constructed around it. That way we could be sure of having enough room. John Rosengrant, who had earlier sculpted the small body, did the large one as well. Welded steel armatures for the legs, arms and head were also constructed; and as they were finished, we put other sculptors on each of those. On the legs we had Graham High and John Robertson. Shane Mahan, who did the miniature head and face, also sculpted the full-size head and face. Chris Overs, Steve Norrington and Philomena Davis were on the large arms; and on the small ones were John Rosengrant, Tom Woodruff and John Robertson. Then I had a large crew - basically the staff moldmakers from Pinewood, headed by Keith Shannon - who worked full-time in my shop making all the casts and the molds.\" To minimize weight, the alien queen was cast in a very light polyfoam - usually directly over the individual armature sections. \"It was especially important that the large arms be as lightweight as possible,\" said Winston. \"As with our foam-core and trashbag mockup, the stuntmen's arms reached only as far as the queen's elbows, so the whole movement of each forearm and hand had to be controlled by the wrist of a person whose own arm was stretched out straight. For the forearms, we again used a ski pole setup - this time with hands that could be either positionable or floppy. Depending on the shot, we would position them in dynamic poses or loosen them up so that they would move around freely when the arms moved. In either case, there was no real articulation as such - but amazingly, the approach worked very well. With all the thrashing around the queen did, it was impossible to tell if the hand movements were free or directed. For our fighting arms, the ski poles were foamed right into the forearm section, which could thus take quite a beating. We also had a set of lighter-weight arms that were polyfoam down to the queen'selbows, but then the forearm was a thin vacuformed shell that weighed practically nothing. Those allowed the stuntmen to have much freer movement - but they were very fragile, so we couldn't use them to bash up against things or else they'd crush.\" For closeups requiring greater dexterity, a pair of insert arms were also constructed. \"The insert arms -which were done by Ray Lovell - had completely articulated fingers cablecontrolled by external operators. These we could use in one of two ways. Either we could position the queen with her elbows out of frame - then come in from the outside with these articulated hands - or we could connect the whole articulated arm right to the queen's body. If we did that, though, we couldn't have our stuntmen inside.\" In addition to operating both sets of arms, the stuntmen were contributors to the queen's tail movement. \"There was hydraulic movement of the tail at the base for ups and downs,\" said Winston, \"but the side-to-side moves depended upon the amount of pressure the stuntmen put on their footplate - which also happened to be the queen's hips. Putting pressure alternately on one side or the other would cause the hips to move from side to side. That, combined with the hydraulic action, created a great deal of base tail movement. At the same time, the outer extremity of the tail was actuated by external wires - usually a combination of two or three wires, each controlled by a different operator.\" During most of the shots, the bulk weight of the queen was supported by a crane arm - either from above by wires or from below via a rigid bracket mount. In both instances, the connecting point was the same. \"The wires and the bracket both attached to a point midway down the queen's back,\" explained Winston. \"The wires were used primarily for the shots where you see her full body. Usually, though, you never see below her knees, and that's where the bracket came in. The bracket came out of the queen's back, down one side of her spine and then under her body where it connected to the crane arm at her pivot axis. The configuration of this bracket - which was built for us by John Richardson and his effects crew - enabled us to shoot the queen without showing the crane arm because the arm connected to the bracket well below the frame line. The bracket itself was also virtually invisible, mainly because we could run it down either side of her back and it would be concealed by her spiny vertebrae. The pivot action that allowed her body to turn was hydraulically controlled by a power steering unit off the crane arm. So an operator, if we wanted the queen to turn from right to left, would simply turn a steering wheel from right to left and the whole body would do the same. Trevor Butterfield was the man in charge of our hydraulics.\" Also hydraulically controlled was a pivot device that enabled the queen to tilt forward and back, and another that created up and down movements for the neck. The head, meanwhile, had its own up and down actuators, as well as a separately controlled left and right function. \"All of these controls went to separate power steering units, each manned by a single operator who had one particular body movement or function to deal with,\" said Winston. \"In the case of the head, we had two slightly different versions. One was our major fighting head - which was built to take abuse - and the other was our 'hero' head which was finer tuned and lighter weight. Functionally, it was about the same as the fighting head, except that it had an extruding tongue mechanism inside and also had tilt capability. In addition to hydraulic controls, the head had cable-actuated functions as well. The face, for example, had its own movement that was independent of the head. The first time the queen is shown, in fact - when Ripley discovers her in the egg chamber - her face extrudes from the head almost like a turtle coming out of its shell. Along with the 360-degree facial movement, there were also cable-operated jaws and snarling lips.\" The queen's legs were actuated in a more straightforward manner. \"Inner articulated armatures were made by Steve Norrington and Richard Landon. In fact, two sets were made. One was for the full legs that were puppeted externally by wires. The other was for a separate set of legs that had no foam below the calf. These were Steve Norrington at work on the most elaborate of the face-hugger puppets. In all, nine operators were required to articulate its cableactuated finger mechanisms, tail whipping action and extruding tongue. / Jim Cameron observes as his actors - and a small army of off-camera puppeteers - rehearse with the fullyarticulated face-hugger that has wrapped its tail around Ripley's neck. Several loosejointed, floppy puppets were also constructed for scenes in which the creatures were thrown across the room and blown up. CINEFEX 27 • 43"],["44","Aliens","Carrying the alien life cycle Cameron devised a gigantic original rendering (below) essential elements would remain intact. As conceived, above and manned by two would articulate the arms. enlisted to create the alien own - including one (top) that could be operated by sitting backwards with his elbow area and his feet in Cameron's concept prevailed. to its logical conclusion, queen whose function it was to perpetuate the species. While his own was to go through an evolutionary process, its it would be operated via hydraulics and cables, supported by wires from intemal operators who When Stan Winston was forms, he sketched out several variants of his only a single stuntman hands in the creature's her knees. Some of Winston's ideas were adopted, but essentially 44 • CINEFEX 27 used for closer shots where the feet were not in frame and could therefore be moved about simply by having operators grab onto the base of the armature and manually step the queen through The queen makes her presence known shortly after the dropship - piloted by Bishop - returns Ripley, Newt and the injured Hicks to the supposed safety of the Sulaco. As Ripley and Bishop converse in the cargo hold, a liquid drop splashes onto the floor between them and begins to sizzle - alien blood! \" For the acid effect,\" said John Richardson, \"I wanted to get away from the old acetone-on-polystyrene look, which is pretty standard. There are other ways of doing it - and we tried some - but in the end, they became so complex in terms of the time it took that we decided they really weren't a viable solution. So we went back to the polystyrene approach, but tried to give it a different look. Instead of acetone, we used carbon tetrachloride - which produces basically the same effect - and we mixed it with all sorts of dyes and soaps and other chemicals so that when it hit not only would it dissolve the polystyrene, but it would smoke and bubble while doing so - which was much better than just seeing something melt through. Also, I found that by adding metallic powder onto the surface of the polystyrene, as it dissolved the powder floated on top of the solution and looked very much like molten metal.\" A split second after the blood hits the deck - and before anyone has a chance to react - the barbed tip of the queen's tail rips through Bishop from behind and lifts him bodily off the floor and up into the drop-ship's landing leg bay where the alien monarch has concealed herself. Seizing the squirming android with both hands, the queen contemptuously rips him in half and casts the pieces aside. \"The Bishop rip-apart was a multistep effect,\" said Winston. \"At first he's just standing there and he gives a little jolt and for an instant you think maybe he's got a chest-burster inside him. Then the tail comes right through him from behind. The normal way to do that effect would be to put him on a slant board with a fake body, but we didn't want to do that. We wanted him to be standing in plain view and then suddenly have this thing shoot out of his chest. To do it, we made a slightly built-up chest plate for Lance Henriksen that allowed a flexible rubberized tail to be inserted and take a bend. So in the start position, the tail piece was lying flat inside the chest plate. It was then pulled up and out through his shirt by a wire that you couldn't see because of the way it was shot - wires can be hidden. Tom Woodruff and Alec Gillis developed that part of the effect. Then we did a switch. The next shot was a variation of the old arrow-through-the-head trick. John Richardson's crew built a harness that went around Lance's back. On the front side was a rigid tail piece that was the length we had pulled out in the previous shot. The back side connected to the queen's actual tail. Lance's feet weren't in the frame, so we were able to put him on a teeterboard to lift him up. Jim set up the shot in such a way that it starts out tight on Lance with the tail sticking through his chest, then widens out to reveal that it is in fact an alien tail that has come through from behind and finally follows that tail all the way up and into the drop-ship where the queen is looming overhead.\" The third phase ofthe effect was by far the most difficult. \"With her tail, the queen lifts Bishop right up to where she is in the drop-ship. Her arms come out. One hand grabs the upper part of his body and the other hand grabs the lower half of his body and she literally rips him in two. Lance said they weren't paying him enough to do that shot, so we had to come up with a dummy rig. That ended up being a team effort between my crew - principally Tom Woodruff and Alec Gillis - and the special effects crew. We built the dummy and matched the expression on its face to the one Lance had had in the previous shot - complete with milk coming out of its mouth. At the same time, the breakaway mechanism was being developed by John Richardson and his people.\" \"Jim wanted Bishop to be held from behind, facing down,\" Richardson explained, \"then be torn in two with a twisting motion so the top half of his body would turn and fall one way and the bottom half would turn and fall the other. My job was to figure out how to hold him up there at the beginning of the shot, where to hold him from, how to release him, when to release him, how to tear one half one way and the other half the other way keeping the creature's hands in place but releasing them at just the right movement. Jim was very adamant about how far the bits of the body had to go and at what angle they should travel. What we finally did was come up with an armature within the dummy - which was already split in half - that would hold everything together until the top and bottom turned a certain number of degrees. Then it would pull apart. At the same time the milky blood material was released with pyrotechnic squibs - one in the head and several in the body. The rest of the effect was done externally with hidden wires. \"One wire attached to the upper left shoulder of the dummy and another one attached to the lower right hip,\" Winston elaborated. \"The queen's hands fit into slots. As the wires were pulled, the body twisted and the spring-loaded armature popped apart - and in doing so, pushed the queen's arms with it so it looked like they were actually doing the tearing. Then the hands released. The final result looked like she was holding him in her hands, ripping him apart and throwing him to the floor. Fortunately, the break was right at the belt line, which allowed us to put him back together, clean up all the milk, give him a new shirt and do it all over again. It took several takes to get everything just right.\" Impaired but not destroyed, Bishop - at least his upper half - would continue to play a key role as the sequence progressed, eventually saving Newt from being sucked into space when the cargo hold undergoes explosive decompression. \"Tom and Alec developed an upper torso with lots of organic and inorganic android innards hanging out. Since Bishop had been tossed to the floor, it was fairly simple to cut a hole in the deck and have Lance's head and arms come up from below with the"],["45","Aliens","fake torso attached to him just under his armpits and over his shoulders.\" Not surprisingly, the scenes where the full-size alien queen had to be shown from head to foot were among the most difficult for Winston and his crew. \"For the scene where she lowers herself down from the drop-ship, we ran wires from the pivot point on her back up through the ship to the top of the stage. Our stuntmen were inside operating the arms and we had wire riggers off to the side lowering the queen down onto the ramp. Other wires connected to the knees and to the ankles allowed the legs to be controlled from above too. It was like a giant marionette. And so the moves wouldn't look floppy, we ran additional wires from her ankles to specific landing points so she could step down firmly into a dynamic position. Her tail also had to uncurl, which meant other wires and other puppeteers. It was pretty rugged. Jim had multiple cameras going and we did it a multitude of times, but the shot was worth it.\" Ripley diverts the giant beast's attention long enough for Newt to scramble safely into a system of trenchlike service channels just below the deck, then runs for one of the giant sliding doors leading out of the cargo hold. The queen is only a step or two behind her, but the heavy door slams shut between them. Turning back, the queen eyes Newt through the grillwork floor and begins pulling up panels to get at her. Suddenly the door slides open to reveal Ripley inside the power-loader, poised and ready for battle. \"In photographing the warriors,\" said Jim Cameron, \"we tried to be circumspect so as not to make them look like men in suits. That was always a concern. With the queen, however, we felt sure that it would hold up because it clearly isn't a person. It has extra limbs and spines and other features that defy normal human geometry, and we therefore knew we could show it longer without blowing the game. All we had to do was make it look nonmechanical - which proved not to be a major problem, given Stan Winston's handiwork and my own tendency to shoot action scenes in very quick cuts. We were even able to speed things up a bit by shooting some of the shots at 12 or 16 frames per second.\" For a full five days, Jim Cameron orchestrated his climactic no-holds-barred battle of the titans - positioning and repositioning his combatants through a carefully choreographed slugfest that ended with both queen and power-loader tumbling into the open airlock and being blown out into space by the unremitting Ripley. \"During the fight,\" said John Richardson, \"there were a lot of different things happening all at once. In fact, for the scenes where we had the alien queen and the power-loader working together, the whole stage was full of special effects people pulling wires and pushing levers. It was quite a sight. Frankly, though, I was a little bit worried about our loader's ability to take all the knocking about it was going to have to take. Being vacuformed plastic, it would have been easy enough to knock out a few spare arms or other bits and pieces, but essentially we'd built only one of them. Fortunately it came through with CINEFEX 27 • 45"],["46","Aliens","46 • CINEFEX 27"],["47","Aliens","flying colors. Nothing got broken. Sigourney Weaver, incidentally, was wonderful. As an effects person, no matter what you do with these kinds of things, in the final anaylsis you're totally at the mercy of the artist. And she couldn't have been better or more helpful. She was very professional and very quick to pick things up and very tolerant in terms of standing on the set for hours at a time doing take after take after take.\" Even though the main unit was approaching the end of its fifteen-week shooting schedule, the effects team still had four months of postproduction yet to go - and all was not running smoothly. Trouble, in fact, was brewing between the production company and L.A. Effects Group. Though not directly involved, Brian Johnson was aware of the situation at Pinewood. A twotime special effects Oscar winner - for the original Alien and for The Empire Strikes Back- Johnson had met with Jim Cameron and Gale Hurd months earlier to discuss his possible involvement as overall purveyor of visual effects for Aliens. When the job later went to L.A. Effects, Johnson assumed that would be the end of it as far as he was concerned. Time would prove him wrong. Despite Cameron's propensity for in-camera effects, it was acknowledged from the beginning that certain scenes - especially those involving long, fluid model moves - would require opticals and motion control photography. To this end, some motion control equipment was leased and transported to Pinewood where it was set up on one of the effects stages. When the system proved inadequate to the task, however, L.A. Effects began examining outside options for obtaining the requisite shots. As the film was being shot in England, it was considered advantageous to engage a local firm - which considerably narrowed the field since motion control technology has not yet proliferated within the British effects industry, Arkadon Motion Control was ultimately selected to do the work - an ideal choice since with four motion control rigs, a full complement of Vistavision cameras and a custom optical printer capable of 4-perf anamorphic and spherical reductions, it was clearly in the forefront technologically. Moreover, it had Brian Johnson - who had founded the company with partners Dennis Lowe and Nick Pollack. \"Originally,\" said Johnson, \"LA. Effects said that they could handle the work, so Jim and Gale hired them. But as time went on, it became obvious that they weren't equipped to handle all of it, so they came to me and asked if we could help them out with the motion control stuff. We agreed to do the work on a subcontract basis. What we did first were four shots for the very beginning of the film showing the Narcissus cruising along and then being sucked up into the salvage ship. They were fairly straightforward motion control shots - just the Narcissus and the salvage ship and a starfield in the background - all done in-camera with bipack composites.\" The two-foot-long Narcissus miniature was actually the first model produced for the sequel. Since its appearance had already been predetermined by the first film, Los Angeles-based modelmaker Jay Roth was commissioned to construct it during the early weeks of preproduction when other design concepts were still very much indefinite and subject to change. \"The original model and any drawings there may have been for it were gone,\" recalled Pat McClung, \"so Jay had to base his work on photographs and frame enlargements from the original film. When he finished with it, it was shipped over to Pinewood where I did a bit more detailing around the window area and part of the interior. I also did a little painting on it, but not very much.\" In the opening shot of the film, the Narcissus would be seen floating aimlessly through space - a tiny speck of human technology barely distinguishable from the stars surrounding it. Then, as the shuttle drifts into the foreground, its wedge-shaped form becomes apparent and the camera tilts downward to follow as it passes underneath. \"In the beginning of the shot,\" said Bob Skotak, \"there's a dense nebula behind the ship. Jim wanted to use that nebula as a means of creating perspective lines that sort of converged. Then, as the camera tilted down, he wanted the feeling of looking into a hole, so there was another nebula with lines going downward.\" Based on Cameron's description, Skotak produced a color sketch that was then turned over to Arkadon where Dennis Lowe produced a final piece of artwork that served as a matted background for the motion control A subsequent shot was to show the Narcissus being drawn into the bowels of the giant salvage ship. \"Jim never wanted to show the salvage ship in its entirety,\" commented McClung. \"All he wanted was this dark ominous shape to engulf the Narcissus - which is exactly what it does. During preproduction, Jim went around with a Camcorder and made video storyboards for most of the effects scenes using very crude little mockups of the ships - just pieces of foam-core and things - and having people walk them through the shots. One weekend, on one of the stages, he found a big jet engine from somewhere that he thought looked interesting. So we hot-glued a lot of little sticks and so forth coming out of this thing and Jim used it as the salvage ship for his video storyboard. And that actually became the basis for the final design - at least the underbelly, which is all you see. Bob Skotak took a four-by-eight piece of plywood - basically tabletop size - and drew out a large opening with a smaller one next to it, made some design sketches and then turned it over to Faisil Karim, one of our modelmakers, to detail it. Faisil went off on his own and after a while came back with a really nice piece of work.\" By year's end - for reasons still embroiled in litigation - discontent between the production company and L.A. Effects had reached critical mass. For Brian Johnson, confirmation of the terminal rift came in the form of a telephone call during his Christmas holiday in the Caribbean. L.A. Effects was off the picture. Would he consider stepping in as effects cosupervisor? Johnson returned to England and signed onto the production. Principal photography was winding down, but the effects unit still had plenty to do. Robert and Dennis Skotak had been In their headlong flight through the lower corridors of the AP station, Ripley and Newt stumble upon a vast egg chamber presided over by the alien queen. As with much of the film, the sequence involved both fullsize and miniature effects. Closeup views of the queen were obtained with a fourteen-foot-tall mechanical creature constructed by Stan Winston's unit. / Jim Cameron and Bob Skotak confer on a small-scale version of the egg chamber set. / Full views of the chamber and scenes showing the distended egg sac and ovipositor were obtained solely with miniatures. The quarter-scale set - which also featured a cable-operated queen constructed by Graham High and Werner Gresty - would be drenched in slime and filled with smoke prior to photography. CINEFEX 27 • 47"],["48","Aliens","In early preproduction, Stan developed a prototype body feasibility of incorporating live performers inside the Warlock and Ed Matthews were the first to try it out, using makeshift extensions next phase was to encase the stunt performers in a mockup queen constructed from black foam-core and plastic trash bags. / Once determined to be workable, which the stuntmen would eventually be placed - was covered with wire mesh and armature. John Rosengrant clay. / Individual sections were worked on by different sculptors. Shane Mahan was charged with producing the refinements and detail to Winston and his people plate suspended from a crane arm to test the queen. Stuntmen Dick to simulate the queen's outstretched arms. / The the basic idea was an aluminum-reinforced fiberglass shell - into constructed and then used as a sculpting applies the first layer of head. / Rosengrant adds the upper torso. 48 • CINEFEX 27 retained from the L.A. Effects Group team and were frantically trying to keep up with the escalating workload. In progress was the drop-ship photography - forty-some shots, none of them easy. Yet to be started were other major scenes involving sectional miniatures of the atmosphere processing station, two differently-scaled versions of the Sulaco's cargo hold, the miniature egg chamber and the rod puppet battle between the power-loader and the alien queen. With Brian Johnson on the team, an informal triumvirate was quickly established. Egos and job titles harmoniously set aside, the work remaining was handled collectively or parceled out to whomever seemed best suited to a particular shot or sequence. \"Aliens is not a mind-bending technical production,\" stated Johnson, \"at least not as far as miniatures and opticals are concerned. The real ground-breaking stuff was in other areas - John Richardson's power-loader and Stan Winston's full-size queen, especially. For the kinds of things we were doing, Jim's whole philosophy was to keep everything simple. If you want a drop-ship flying over a planet, you build a miniature drop-ship and you build a miniature landscape and you fly the thing by on wires. Nevertheless, the drop-ship shots were among the most difficult and time-consuming from the point of view of the scale that we were shooting in and the fact that Jim was progressively speeding up the action. We knew that with models on wires and miniature settings it would be necessary to shoot at high-speed to smooth out the moves and create a believable sense of mass. The problem was that Jim wanted the action to be a lot faster than originally envisioned, I think. As a result, the models - and sometimes the camera - had to be moving really fast to compensate for the fact that what we were actually doing was photographing in slow motion. On some of the shots, we were running as high as 120 frames per second. We needed to get a feeling of moving through the set and the only way to do that was to go as fast as we could and then progressively lower the frame rate until we found something that was acceptable. There were some terribly fast tracking maneuvers. Unfortunately, we didn't have time to devise any major rigs to accomplish these kinds of moves. All we could do was adapt what we had.\" The principal flying rig for the drop-ship was constructed by senior effects technician Jonathan Angell. \"When that rig was put together,\" said Bob Skotak, \"all we had was a sense from talking to Jim of what he wanted. Some of the shots weren't storyboarded - at least in final form - and our schedules were such that we couldn't afford the time to sit down with a piece of paper and engineer this thing with any great detail. So we improvised. We made a few quick mental calculations and then Jonathan Angell and I went out and started looking around at what was available at the studio. We looked at pipes and base rigs that Derek Meddings had and what we ended up with was pretty much assembled from existing elements. The boom arm for the flying rig we found sitting on the ground with weeds growing up around it. In fact, there were two of them in the same place and we sort of jiggled them and said, 'Well, this one will"],["49","Aliens","CINEFEX 27 • 49"],["50","Aliens","50 • CINEFEX 27"],["51","Aliens","move around a little faster and it's a little longer, but this one will fit into the set better' - I mean, that's about how scientific we were getting. So a lot of it was just gut feeling. And as it turned out, it was exactly what we needed.\" \"Essentially, the rig consisted of a metal platform that was about six feet high,\" Dennis Skotak explained. \"On top of that platform was a twelve-foot-long boom arm from which the sixfoot drop-ship was hung on wires. Cables ran up from the model to a steering mechanism that Jonathan Angell worked out. That mechanism enabled the nose or tail of the drop-ship to lift and also gave it a rotational move. Two guys - usually John Brown and Darryl Guyon - maneuvered the boom arm to get the thing to turn in place and bank and do all those sorts of quasihelicoptor moves that Jim wanted. Then the whole rig rode on a track, which gave us our linear movement. It would take two to six guys - depending on how much speed we needed - to push the platform down the track. Then, when they got to the end, they'd have to put the 'brakes' on - which amounted to digging their heels into the ground. One of the hardest things was getting it to lift off with the boom, make an elongated arc and then phase out of that and into a linear move.\" \"Another thing that was always a problem was the momentum that would build up as it moved down the track,\" Bob Skotak added. \"When we wanted it to stop, we'd have to bring the whole rig to a gradual slow-down so that it stayed up with the wire. If we didn't, we'd end up overshooting our mark and the whole thing would crash into the miniature set - which happened more than once. There were also mechanical problems to deal with - getting the lights of the ship to come on when they were supposed to and things like that. Plus we were overcranking - on the average, about 48 frames per second - and since Jim wanted everything to happen fast, that meant that the moves actually had to be done at double whatever the speed was that he wanted. That was difficult. It's amazing how fast you have to do these things even to make them look slow on film. It took a lot of rehearsal. Fortunately, the rig Jonathan pieced together had exactly the amount of arc and exactly the amount of linear move that we needed. And we used it for probably twenty-some shots - coming in over the colony complex and landing, taking off to rescue the people who were stranded, coming into the AP station, landing on the platform and taking off again - so it was quite a busy little rig.\" Nowhere were the skills of the wire crew more heavily taxed than in the scenes of the drop-ship flying through the selfdestructing AP station. The interior maw set was comprised of two large pieces, each fifteen feet tall by about thirty feet long and each constructed at slightly different scales so they could be repositioned into various forced perspective configurations. \"Essentially it was a scaffolding rig,\" said Bob Skotak, \"which was fortunate because they tend to be strong and the design kind of lent itself to that. Peter Russell - who was a liaison with the art department - oversaw the construction. Basically it was a combination of plastic and metal pipes with a few silo tubes stuck around here and there. There were also a lot of wooden dowels and blocks cut in various ways - half-rounds and cylinders and so forth. And then a lot of little detailing pieces - ladders and ramps and that sort of thing, It was a major joint effort on the part of the modelmakers and the riggers and the carpenters - and it worked well. We got a lot of shots out of it. Plus it was pretty sturdy. We must have put five hundred explosions through that set - some of them pretty big bombs - and at the end it was still standing.\" \"Just flying it through was not all that difficult,\" recalled Dennis Skotak. \"What was difficult was getting the drop-ship to land. The ship had to swing around in just the right way, come in close to the camera and then drop down onto the landing platform. Just doing that smoothly and hitting all the marks was hard enough, but since we were overcranking the camera everything had to be done much faster than normal speed which made it really tough on the crew - especially John and Darryl who were on top of the flying rig while it was being pushed around. You have to remember this was hand-operated, not controlled by a computer. What was even worse was a scene later on when Ripley and Newt come back up to the landing platform and everything around them is exploding. We needed a plate for that in which the drop-ship rises into the frame, hits its mark and then does a helicopter-like drifting maneuver. With explosions going off all over the place, it was really hard for the operators to hit their marks. Everybody was a bit jumpy. But there was no room for error. I suppose you could have done a shot like that much simpler with separate elements, but I don't think it would have had the same interaction, the feeling of all the elements interplaying with one another.\" The drop-ship's last-minute arrival would signal the climax of a major live-action sequence created almost exclusively with rear-projected miniature backgrounds. \"Nearly every scene with Ripley and the girl going up the elevator used rear projection,\" affirmed Brian Johnson. \"They're in the cage and in the background you can see the station and explosions going off. Plates reat precion of. Pales for those scenes worked really well as rear projection - mainly because the flames and smoke that were pumped in over the foreground degraded the overall image to the point where it effectively camouflaged any problems in the background you might normally have gotten with straight rear-projection. Plus Jim uses fast action and quick cuts and moves his camera a lot. Obviously, if you have a locked-off camera and people standing around in front of a process screen it's always going to look terrible. But with Jim, he pans his camera and shakes it and blows dust around in front of it - and by doing that, he's able to take the curse off rear projection.\" When principal photography wrapped in late January, the production shut down for a week while Jim Cameron worked with editor Ray Lovejoy on formulating a rough-cut of the film. Then, on a small stage adjacent to the production offices, Cameron supervised an additional three weeks of postproduction Armature specialist Ray Lovell threads actuating cables through the queen's six-finger hand mechanism. / Keith Shannon - head mold maker assigned to the creature unit - lays clay in around a completed hand sculpture as a preliminary step in the moldmaking process. / Sculptor Christine Overs at work on one of the arms. Since the arms were to be operated by stuntmen concealed within the queen's torso - and since their arms reached only to her elbow area - it was imperative that the extended forelimbs be as maneuverable as possible. For the fight scenes, the forearms were made from lightweight but durable polyfoam: for less physical scenes, an even lighter vacuformed shell was employed. CINEFEX 27 • 51"],["52","Aliens","On location at the power station, the full-size queen addition to the manually operated arms, numerous hydraulic and cable-operated mechanisms were employed to achieve required body and facial movements. / Physical quarter-scale model of the which he used in working support rig. / In most of the shots, the queen's weight bracket mount that attached midway down her back and then ran under her body where it attached to a crane arm. Since most of the shots knees, the support device plant serving as the atmosphere processing is readied for a shot. In effects supervisor John Richardson studies a queen - constructed by Stan Winston's unit - out plans for an overall was suspended from a were framed above her was generally out of camera range. 52 • CINEFEX 27 effects and insert photography - juggling as many as four or five different setups at a time. Among the scenes remaining were those within the cargo hold that were too expansive to be done on the partial full-scale set or those requiring articulation of the drop-ships. For these shots, a twelfth-scale miniature of the cargo hold was constructed - an elevated mini-set eight feet wide by twenty feet deep - precisely matched to its full-size counterpart and equipped with two six-foot drop-ships. Conveniently, the script had been written in such a way that no human beings were present during either launch sequence - a factor which simplified matters considerably for the effects crew. \"In the film,\" said Bob Skotak, \"the drop-ship legs come up, the doors in the floor beneath it open and the ship descends into a giant airlock. Telescoping tubes attached to overhead tracks were motorized so the ship could be lowered at different speeds. The lights and landing legs were operated by radio control and the doors of the airlock were opened manually from below. It was all pretty straightforward.\" In addition to the launch scenes, the twelfth-scale cargo-hold would also be used for a shot showing the armored personnel carrier backing up the drop-ship ramp prior to departure. As with most of the miniature shots in the film, the cargo hold scenes were photographed in a smoke environment to duplicate the aerial texture Cameron had created by smoking up his live-action sets during the main unit shoot. \"One of the toughest things throughout was matching smoke densities,\" Bob Skotak noted. \"We had to anticipate which takes from the live-action Jim would be most likely to use and then try to match our smoke level to that particular take. We'd see the shots in dailies, then come out to the stage and say, 'Well, this amount of smoke looks about right.' And it was very hard to control because there are many things that determine whether it's going to match up or not.\" Across the stage, a quarter-scale set representing the egg chamber was also being readied. Though live-action portions of the sequence had been shot with the full-size mechanical queen, the actual egg-laying process and the chamber in its entirety would be seen only in miniature. Dominating the eggfilled set and framed by a vast spiny crown was the alien queen herself - a cable-actuated puppet perched upon an enormous abdominal distention slung pendulously from the overhead ductwork. Scattered about on the floor were dozens of vacuformed eggs ranging from eight to ten inches in height. \"It was a very difficult environment to work in,\" said Dennis Skotak, \"and as usual, Jim was very specific about how everything had to be. The egg sac had to operate in a certain manner, the eggs had to move in just such a way and the ovipositor had to deposit an egg precisely and with just the right amount of goo. To create a breathing effect, we had wood poles on the sac leading away from the camera and wires to move the eggs inside. Then, to get the ovipositor to work, someone had to reach way over through an opening in the side of the set, slide his hand into the egg sac from the rear and guide the egg out. One of the biggest problems was that there was so much slime in there that we'd get an egg all positioned and ready to go and it would keep popping out before we got to shoot it. So someone would have to climb back in and reposition it - and it got messier and more disgusting every time. In fact, everything about that set was unpleasant. There was K-Y jelly and 'superslime' dribbling all over the place and it was extremely hot. We had steam rising from below and smoke and all kinds of smelly things in there - even cans of freon spurting from both sides. It was like shooting After a momentary standoff in which the alien adversaries size each other up, Ripley discharges her grenade launcher into the giant pulsating egg sac. \"That was another messy one,\" Skotak continued, \"especially since we found that the most dynamic way of shooting it was up close, with a fairly wide lens, so the sac exploded right into the camera. We'd get everything all set up and then blow the sac apart with charges and all of this goo would come splurting out and collect in a bucket down below so we could recycle it. For some reason, shooting that scene always drew an audience. We'd warn people to keep their distance, but invariably someone would wind up with a face full of slime.\" As explosions and flame rip through the egg chamber, the vengeful queen tears herself free from the ruptured egg sac. \"It wasn't possible to do that in full-size, so we were faced with the difficulty of dealing with flame in scale. Bob came up with the idea of using mirrors. One of them was placed behind the queen and positioned in such a way to reflect a flame bar we had set up about fifteen feet away. At that distance, the flames - which were about six or seven feet high - looked quite small in the mirror. It was a good scale. In the same shot, another flame bar was positioned similarly, but in such a way that when photographed through a beam-splitter, flames would also appear to be in front of the queen. Obviously, we could have done the same effect optically, but this method allowed us to move the camera during the shot and also introduce mechanical effects and falling debris in front of the background flames - and do it all quite simply.\" Elsewhere on the stage, a quarter-scale version of the cargo hold set - complete with drop-ship - had been erected for miniature scenes required to augment the full-size battle between the power-loader and the alien queen. As determined during preproduction, either stop-motion or rod puppet versions of the combatants would be employed - possibly both. In either event, Cameron's natural inclination was to approach Doug amer had ben rest Beswick who had been responsible for constructing the stopmotion robot used in The Terminatorand whose company had just completed a highly-successful rod-and cable-actuated tyrannosaurus rex for My Science Project. A meeting was arranged shortly before Cameron's departure for England and Doug BesWith a comfortable budget and a six-month deadline, Beswick began marshaling his forces. Principal among them was mechanical systems designer Phil Notaro who had developed the artic-"],["53","Aliens","CINEFEX 27 • 53"],["54","Aliens","54 - CINEFEX 27"],["55","Aliens","ulation mechanisms for the Science Project saurian and would be doing likewise for the Aliens characters. Other major participants would include cosmetics supervisor Tony Gardner and construction supervisor Jim Belohovek. Though the job as first stated was to build both stop-motion and rod puppet versions of the characters, budgetary considerations would ultimately - and in short order - terminate the stop-motion requirement. \"We started working on the alien queen first,\" Beswick explained. \"At the time, Stan Winston had a whole army of guys sculpting his quarter-scale version, which he was then going to take to London as a model for the full-scale one. Our puppet was going to be the same size as Stan's miniature model - about three feet tall - so while they were still in the process of sculpting, Phil would go over there and take measurements and photographs of it so that he could begin working on some of the early mechanical designs.\" Much like the tyrannosaurus rex, the alien queen would be supported by a rigid pole that extended out from the base of her spine and into a maneuverable overhead crossbar. Also like the tyrannosaurus rex, her basic ambulatory movements would be effected via rods that attached to her feet and ran down through slots in the floor of the set. Unlike the tyrannosaurus rex, however, there was practically no space inside the queen to put the multitude of mechanisms and cables required to achieve her more subtle articulations. \"From a mechanical perspective, the design was very difficult - mainly because the queen was extremely complex and extremely skinny. During that first month when the model was still being sculpted, Phil was able to do some of the mechanics we'd be needing, but not a whole lot because the dimensions weren't totally locked in yet. In fact, we ended up having to do a couple of things over - like the back joint. Phil underestimated how deep they were going to sculpt the undercuts all around the thorax and, as a result, when it was finished the mechanism he'd worked out for it didn't fit. So he had to cut it way down, and even then it just barely made it.\" \"It was a tight fit,\" Notaro confirmed. \"The thorax narrowed to a tiny triangle-shaped area that was maybe three inches from corner to corner. That in itself wasn't bad, but because the queen had so many mechanical functions we ended up having to run forty-nine cables through that area - through a hole that was only about an inch-and-a-half around. Complicating things further was the fact that the queen was going to have to bend at the waist. As a result, we had to put in a massive joint that would enable her to bend with all those cables in there, plus an inch of foam all the way around. It was important that we have a lot of control over the body. Most especially, it had to be solid enough to hold its position so that when the head was slashing back and forth, the body wouldn't follow it. The arms were also a problem. They were only three-quarters of an inch in diameter on the outside, yet they had to have nine functions each. The arms and shoulders moved up and down, forward and back, and rotated. The elbows and wrists bent, the forearms rotated, and even though they were smaller than a soda straw - about threesixteenths of an inch in diameter - all of the fingers had to be functional. We kept trying to talk Jim into either simplifying the queen or fattening her up a bit but he was very adamant that we stick to the design. It got to be kind of funny because we'd go to him and say, 'Couldn't the fingers be a little bigger?' And he'd say, 'That's not the design.' Or we'd say, 'There's not enough room for a mechanism in here.' And he'd say, 'But that's the design.' Everything we asked for, we didn't get. In the end, the alien queen turned out exactly as Jim had planned it in the first place.\" With no choice but to adhere to the complex design, Notaro constructed a basic aluminum armature and then proceeded to mechanize it. \"As each new mechanism went in, space became very tough to come by. I'd lay out a game plan for where I could put something - usually in some tiny little space somewhere - and then I'd have to go in and shift everything all around to actually make it work. It was a long, continuous process. By the time I finished, there was virtually no room left inside at all.\" As planned, the puppet queen would be a more-than-capable performer. \"The head was able to move left and right, nod and turn. Independently, the face could also turn left and right, and had jaws that could open and an extending tongue. We also gave her a lower neck function that had two movements - up and down, and left and right. The arms had three-function shoulders, elbows and wrists, and the fingers were articulated. Then she had spines that moved up and down, and little arms in the front that had up and down movement. Beyond that, the wrists were just floppy so the hands would wiggle back and forth as the arms moved. We also gave her an animated tail which was very difficult to work with because it was just a big sculpted piece and it was very heavy. We tried and tried to get smooth, fluid movements from it but we eventually had to hold it up by wires to take some of the weight off of it and get it to work.\" Hoping to minimize the number of puppeteers required, Notaro consolidated related cable functions so that they could be operated via a single joystick. \"Oftentimes the difference between jerky, unnatural-looking movement and fluid, naturallooking movement is the way the functions have been organized for operation. When I hook up these kinds of things, I try to group the cables together in such a way that one puppeteer can handle several functions. But they have to be interrelated functions. On the queen, for example, the head and neck and face functions were all fed into one set of joysticks so that just one operator could control everything needed to give the character expression. That's the only way to get a real flow in the character. You don't want to have one operator controlling two different functions; nor do you want to have one function operated by a whole bunch of different people. It just makes sense. If the functions are interrelated, you can get a better feeling for the character and make it look more realistic. It also means less breakdown in communication. If you want something to happen, you tell one person as opposed to telling three or four and then trying to choreograph their movements.\" Ultimately, the queen's fortySurviving members of the assault team have only a moment to relish their safe passage to the Sulaco when the queen - a stowaway in one of the landing leg wells in the drop-ship - suddenly lowers her tail and thrusts it through the android Bishop. / To achieve the shot, a lowprofile chest plate was fitted under Lance Henriksen's shirt. A channel in the plate allowed for the insertion of a flexible rubber tail piece with a pull-wire on the tip and a rod handle down below out of camera range. On cue, the tail was thrust upward and out through the shirt. In preparation for a take, Stan Winston adjusts the full tail attached to Henriksen's back while Tom Woodruff awaits his cue to push the protruding end upward. / For a subsequent scene in which the queen draws Bishop up into the drop-ship and then rips him in half, a dummy version of the android was prepared by the creature unit and then fitted over a breakaway armature devised and constructed by the physical effects crew. CINEFEX 27 • 55"],["56","Aliens","Having disposed of Bishop, the queen lowers herself from the drop-ship. Though stuntmen were still inside supported bracket rig could not be used for full-figure shots. Instead, wires were attached to her back that ran shuttlecraft to the top of the connected to her knees and ankles so that they too were puppeteered from above. To avoid floppy marionette-type moves, additional wires ran from her ankles to specific landing points so she could were rendered invisible to operating the arms, the unconcealable craneup through the mockup stage and then down to operators positioned off camera. Other lines step down firmly into a dynamic position. With careful lighting and generous amounts of smoke, actuating wires the camera. 56 • CINEFEX 27 nine actuating cables would feed into ten joystick mechanisms operated by five on-set puppeteers. Four other technicians would also be required - two to support and direct the queen from above, and two to operate the legs from below. While Notaro was concentrating on the mechanical aspects ofthe queen, Tony Gardner - assisted by Brian Penikas, Shannon Shea and Kevin Yagher - was at work producing the multitude of molds from which her outer form would be cast. \"Stan Winston's people had made silicone RIV molds from their quarterscale clay sculpture,\" said Beswick, \"but for our purposes, we needed stone molds. I find that stone molds are generally better when you're working with armatures because they give you something solid to work with. Also, you get better castings out of them. I've never had much luck getting good skins from an RTV mold. So Tony Gardner went in and made clay press-outs from the original molds, cleaned up the sculpture from that process and then remade the molds in Ultracal so that we could run our latex pieces.\" Once the stone molds were ready, Michael Burnett ran the latex foam skins that would be pieced together over the aluminum armature. \"Putting the body on was difficult,\" Notaro related, \"because we had to stretch the foam over all of this metal. Usually, we have a fiberglass substructure in our figures, but in this case there was no room for one. So the foam had to be thick enough that we wouldn't have metal things poking through it, yet thin enough to still bend and fit the character. We had a core that was put inside to hollow out the foam body, but by the time I was done putting in all the mechanics, Tony had to literally cut the foam down to paper thinness in some spots.\" Not all of the outer structure would be foam, however. For the massive swept-back head, something rigid and more durable was required. \"We were especially worried about the head,\" Beswick confessed, \"because it was about eighteen inches long - which was huge in relation to the tiny neck joint it had to rest on. So it had to be very light, yet strong enough to make it through shooting. We made it out of the thinnest fiberglass we've ever used - about thirty-thousandths of an inch thick. We used a little bit of gel coat, then one layer of angel hair, one layer of half-ounce cloth and a very little bit of resin. We did it in two sections - the top and bottom separately - and then seamed them together. Amazingly, it was very strong and it held up through the filming like a trooper.\" Fully erect, the finished queen stood about three feet tall. \"She was actually much taller than the power-loader in her normal stance,\" revealed Notaro, \"but was crouched most of the time because she was still suffering from having her egg sac removed. She had two knee joints, one of which was a friction joint that we could pose to make her taller or shorter depending the station. The quite on the situation. The queen was also considerably heavier than the power-loader because she had so much stuff inside her. For one thing, she had more than twice as many cables, and the cable housing itself is probably one of the heaviest parts of the character.\" Despite her ponderous weight, the queen was quite capable of quick, darting movements. \"One of Jim Cameron's comments when he saw our dinosaur from My Science Project,\" said Beswick, \"was that the alien queen would have to move very fast - almost like a blur. The dinosaur had to move very slowly, which was actually more difficult. It's really much easier to get smooth movement from something that's moving quickly than it is from something that's moving slowly. I think Jim got the effect he wanted from the alien queen - quick, yet fluid action.\" Almost from the beginning, Beswick's production team had had the benefit of a fully-realized concept for the alien queen to guide them in building the puppet. For the power-loader puppet, however, they would not be so fortunate. Although Jim Cameron had devised a basic look for the loader during preproduction, specific details of its design could not be pinned down until the engineering and logistical problems of constructing the full-size one had been resolved. Further complicating matters was the fact that the full-scale work was being done at Pinewood, leaving Beswick and Notaro completely dependent on sketches and photographs from abroad to guide them. \"We'd get scaled pictures of what they were building in England and we'd start building from those,\" Notaro recalled, \"and then we'd get to the point where we had to stop until we could get more information. It was an extremely slow process. Usually, there was a turnover time of about a week just to get the pictures from there to here. Then we'd have to look at them and try to figure out what they were doing over there and then build it in miniature. We started off with the arms, then went to the legs and finally to the body - which is the way they were doing it. Usually, I like to start with the main frame of the figure just to get the overall feel of it. The power-loader necessitated a different approach - which wasn't necessarily difficult, but it was a little more time-consuming. The puppet itself was actually quite simple. It was only the evolution of it that was slow. In the end, I had to finish it up when I got to England. They were working on the full-size one right up until the time they started shooting it, so I actually couldn't finish the puppet until I got over there and saw it -'Aha! That's what it looks like!' Then I was able to put the fine details on it. It was pretty tense for a while there, though, because we were halfway into our schedule and we hadn't even started the power-loader yet.\" Like the miniature queen, the power-loader was to be a quarterscale armatured version of the live-action vehicle. \"Essentially I started with as full-size a sketch as I could come up with,\" Notaro said. \"John Richardson sent us blueprints of the overall size and dimensions of the thing and from that I started building the internal mechanisms and working out the details. Most of the movement was in the arms. It had wrist movement and elbow movement and shoulder movement - and it had grippers that had to open and close. The wrist was only one-directional. The other direction had a friction-type joint so you could position it in whatever way you wanted. Another function it had was torso movement - the mid-torso could twist back and forth to give"],["57","Aliens","it side-to-side mobility. Those were about the only functions we could give the character because of its design - it was very cut and dried. The legs were just free-animated so that they could be controlled through the floor by rods to make it walk.\" While Phil Notaro assembled the intricate internal workings of the power-loader, Jim Belohovek was making the patterns from which its outer shape would be fashioned. \"Unlike the alien queen, which was a sculpted form,\" commented Beswick, \"the power-loader was constructed like a model because of its symmetrical configuration and its hard, flat lines. Working from the photos and drawings we were getting from London, Jim made patterns for all the basic shapes out of plastic, bondo and wood. From those, Tony Gardner made silicone RTV molds with stone backings - and those molds, in turn, were used to produce a fiberglass outer shell for the loader.\" \"Once I finished the aluminum substructure,\" noted Notaro, \"we attached the fiberglass pieces to the main frame. To keep everything as light as possible, the fiberglass was held up from the mechanism by little aluminum standoffs - so it was essentially hollow. Then the pieces were seamed together - either glued together and patched, or a trim piece was put over the seam to make it look like it was one solid piece. Each piece built on top of another so that by the time it was finished, it was one pretty solid unit. We used a very thin fiberglass for the outer shell because puppets have a tendency to become too heavy. The lighter a puppet is, the more responsive it is and the easier it is to handle and operate. As it turned out, however, we probably made the structure lighter than it should have been. We didn't know at that time that it was going to be abused as badly as it was - Jim Cameron really got carried away with those puppets once he had them on the set.\" The finished powerloader - nearly thirty inches tall - was topped off with a rotating beacon to match the light atop the full-scale vehicle. \"It was just a halogen bulb with a little pyrex dome that was dyed amber and stuck on top. We were a little concerned about how it would hold up, but the rotating beacon turned out to be the most durable part of the puppet.\" As with the queen, the power-loader functions were controlled by joysticks, requiring one puppeteer to control the body movement and four more to operate the arms - two on each. Fortunately, its stocky build helped to hide the cables which actuated it. \"The legs of the power-loader were quite wide,\" Notaro remarked, \"and, luckily, most of the shots of it were from the side or in three-quarter profile. Very few were dead straight on. As a result, we were able to drop the cables down through the butt and wrap them around the support post that came down between the legsand into the floor. Then, by painting them either yellow - which was the basic color of the loader - or whatever color the background happened to be, they completely disappeared. You'd never know they were there.\" The quarter-scale power-loader also needed a quarter-scale Ripley figure to operate it. \"Ripley was kind of the unknown puppet,\" said Notaro, \"but she starred right along with the other CINEFEX 27 • 57"],["58","Aliens","58 - CINEFEX 27"],["59","Aliens","ones. Stuart Land sculpted the figure, working from photographs, and she was put together very quickly - the mechanism for it only took me two days to build. Margaret Beserra made a miniature wig for the figure, and Teresa Burkett made the miniature costume. During the fight scene, the Ripley puppet fit inside the power-loader and had free-animated arms which attached to the pistol grips on both sides. It was supposed to look like she was operating the power-loader, but what we did was actually the reverse - we moved the loader arms and Ripley's attached arms just followed. She also had head movements which were controlled by the same puppeteer who was doing the torso turn on the power-loader. That way, when the power-loader turned, Ripley's head would turn in the same direction, giving a flow to all the action.\" Redressed with a beard and cap, the Ripley puppet would also be featured earlier in the film. \"In the first cargo hold sequence,\" Bob Skotak explained, \"when everyone's loading things into the drop-ship, Jim wanted to establish two functioning powerloaders, but there'd only been one built for the film. So the plan was to shoot a plate of the loader working on the cargo line and then put Sigourney Weaver in the loader and project the plate up behind her. But there wasn't time to do it, so the shot was dropped. Then, during the very last week of the schedule - when the main unit had shut down and everybody but Sigourney was gone - Jim had us set up the power-loader and we shot some footage of Sigourney in front of a blue screen. Since the main set had already been struck, we then staged a scene with the puppet power-loader on the miniature cargo hold set and used that for the background plate. It was the only live-action bluescreen shot in the film.\" Due to a shortage of work permits, only one member of the Beswick team would be allowed to accompany the completed puppets to England. Since he was most familiar with the mechanisms and most prepared in the event on-set repairs were needed, Phil Notaro was elected to go. Actual puppeteers would be drawn from the resident ranks of modelmakers and creature crew members whose primary work had already been completed. \"When I first got there,\" said Notaro, \"the puppets had arrived, but none of the tools or controls had. So my first week was basically devoted to last-minute detailing on the power-loader and coordinating with Jim as to what he wanted. I also spent a lot of time with Barry Saunders, the construction manager. The quarter-scale cargo hold set had already been built - and it was a beautiful job - but basically it was not very accommodating to our needs. It was a lot of pipes and wood and things, all nailed down very securely into place. Since we were going to be operating the puppets from below the floor, we had to cut big slots in the set and then cosmetically disguise them. So to get our slots where we needed them, there was a lot of sawing and cutting and spray-painting that had to be done.\" On the average, Notaro and his unit were to generate one shot during each day of his three-week stint at Pinewood. \"Just setting up was a major production. For one thing, each shot was really two shots because we had two different camera angles on most of them. Whenever we went to set up a puppet we had to cut these slots in the floor and then reinforce everything, which was very time-consuming. Also, we had to set up the video assist every time - we had a camera feed and a secondary camera so the guys below the floor could see what was happening. Most of the puppeteers were sitting at floor level; but I also had people above the set, so there had to be another monitor up top. Also, we were shooting with virtually no storyboards. Nobody really had any idea what Jim wanted, except for him. So I had to take everything with me to the set, every day. Sometimes I'd try to second-guess him - but every time I did, sure enough, he'd want something other than what we'd set up for. He'd ask how long it would take me to get ready and I'd say, 'Oh, two hours.' And he'd say, 'Okay, I'll be back in an hour and fortyfive minutes.' And an hour and a half later he'd be back saying, 'Are we ready?' He always had to cut half an hour off of your estimate. It was rough going, because I'd usually have to rehang and redo all the support posts and mechanisms. As time went on, we tended to do longer shots with fewer setups - longer shots of each so that the actual number of shots was cut down. Jim had gotten more usable stuff from the live-action characters than he'd anticipated, so he didn't need as many shots with the puppets.\" First to be photographed was a scene of the queen racing toward camera in three giant strides, trying to intercept Ripley before she reaches the sanctuary of the sliding cargo hold door. \"We had everybody working on that shot,\" said Notaro. \"Two guys supporting the queen from above, two others walking her from below and five cable operators for the other functions. In all, the queen was capable of about thirty movements, but Jim wanted one more when we got to shooting - a rotating move right at the top of the tail so that she'd be able to turn more quickly. So we added a tiny mechanism in her butt that allowed her whole body to turn around farther and faster. That was also helped along by turning the post she was mounted on. By turning the post, everything turned.\" After exhaustive choreography and rehearsal, smoke was introduced into the set, rotating beacon lights were turned on and the puppeteers went through their paces. \"Jim wanted all the action to be very fast - and since we were shooting at 36 frames per second, that meant moving extra fast so it would still look quick when we played it back normal speed. We went through a lot of takes, but finally In his pursuit of dramatic effect, Cameron showed no mercy to the puppet, constantly pushing the puppeteers to move faster and harder. \"During our next shot, we had two puppeteers literally ramming the puppet into the sliding doors as hard as they could - over and over again. Then, after about ten takes, Jim would say, 'Here's how I want you to do it.' And he'd smash it three times harder than anybody else had! Luckily, the queen turned out to be very durable. We did have some slippages in the mechanisms, but that was to be expected since they had Creature crew members Alec Gillis, John Rosengrant and David Keen adjust the rigging and cable mechanisms for the queen's descent from the drop-ship. / Stan Winston and Shane Mahan perform cosmetic touch-ups while other crew members support the queen's arms, thus relieving the stuntmen sealed inside. / Suited up in the powerloader, Ripley reenters the cargo hold to do battle with the queen. While Sigoumey Weaver was the apparent operator, her efforts were augmented considerably by stuntman-weightlifter John Lees who was concealed just inches behind her within the loader's outer shell. Additional support for the six-hundred-pound machine was provided via wires attached to an overhead crane arm. Lightweight radio-control mechanisms in the arms allowed for wrist swivel and claw rotation, while the grippers themselves were controlled by cables. CINEFEX 27 • 59"],["60","Aliens","dismembered android was developed by Tom Woodruff and Alec Gillis, then fitted over Lance Henriksen whose own body and legs extended through a hole in a deck. / Ripley fends off her attacker in a battle to the death. / The queen and the powerloader with their respective external actuators. Since the scene being photographed featured only the upper half of the loader, its mechanical legs were removed to afford greater maneuverability for operators Sigoumey Weaver and John Lees. A platform A severed torso for the down and out of sight support systems and was then erected to compensate for the difference in height. 60 4 CINEFEX 27 been built to fit into a very thin, small area and they were being abused very badly. I was constantly adjusting things to keep it operating correctly. We also had to keep it looking good because we might do a scene one day where she was completely battered and the next day do a scene where she had to look brand new. There was constant maintenance going on.\" After her unsuccessful attempt to break through the cargo hold door, the miniature queen would not be seen again until later in the sequence when she would be called upon to grapple with the miniature power-loader in a few key shots the main unit had been unable to get. \"Where Ripley wallops the queen with the power-loader arms, then grabs her by the neck and lifts her into the air - that was all done live-action,\" said Notaro. \"We picked it up with the puppets at that point, just as Ripley is about to toss the queen into the airlock. We had a couple shots of them fighting, then the queen breaks one of the hydraulics on the right knee of the power-loader and it buckles up, sending both of them crashing to the bottom of the airlock.\" Since no articulation was required during the fall, a floppy stand-in was employed for the queen. \"We used the floppy queen for a lot of the tumbling and falling sequences. It was just hard and soft foam pieces glued together. We'd throw the floppy queen over a bunch of boxes, for example, then cut back to the articulated queen for when she stood back up. Unfortunately, we only had one power-loader, so we had to use the articulated one no matter what the scene called for. For the shot of it crashing into the airlock, we took it off its post, disconnected all the cables and just hung it up on wires and controlled it like a marionette. At that point, Jim wasn't concerned with fine, subtle movement. He just wanted the gross movement as it fell into the airlock. The puppets actually dropped about five feet - and as they fell, the power-loader would wiggle and then crash at the bottom, which is what he was looking for. We did about eight takes on it, and each time it crashed into pieces. We'd then have to superglue it back together and do it again.\" Ripley somehow survives the fall and manages to crawl up a ladder to the switch that will open the outer airlock doors and suck the queen out into space - a method which experience has taught her is practically the only way to dispose of the alien creatures. \"After we had done the shot of the queen and the power-loader falling onto the lower set of doors, we took the airlock out of the cargo hold set and bolted it to the ceiling which was twenty-five or thirty feet in the air. We then positioned the puppets, opened the airlock doors and let them fall out. Fortunately, we got it on the first take - because instead of falling onto a big starfield blanket we had down below, the power-loader hit the edge of the starfield, went through, struck the concrete floor beneath and shattered into a million pieces. At that point, there was nothing to do but put it in a box. But Jim was very pleased with it. He looked over the edge as it was being filmed and said: 'That's it! We don't have to do thatagain.' Which, under the circumstances, was a good thing. Ironically, though, after being smashed and beaten and demolished for three weeks, our little rotating glass beacon on the top was still working.\" Phil Notaro's term at Pinewood was drawing to a close, but there remained yet another brief encounter with the alien queen. \"We needed a shot earlier in the AP station when Ripley is running toward the lift and the queen is chasing after her. The idea was that the queen was really too big to fit in the corridor very well and that's how Ripley gets ahead. for us, it was a difficult shot in that she had to be crushed down to much shorter than her usual height and then squeezed through a quarter-scale set representing the hallway. To make it work, I readjusted the support post so that rather than coming out of her back and up, it came out and to the rear, away from camera. Then I disconnected the joysticks and ran all the cables through slots I had cut in the post. She had to travel about six feet down the corridor, so I put the post with the cables on it on a camera dolly that I'd rented and then reconnected the joysticks. It was still a very tight squeeze. Even shortened to her minimum height, she just barely fit in the hallway. I managed to get everything all set up and ready to go before my flight home. Then, after I left, they shot it for two days - the Pinewood people and some of the others who'd been working the puppets all along - and The Aliens special effects marathon would draw to a close on the motion control stages at Arkadon, Brian Johnson's facility in rural Bourne End where the Narcissus had been committed to film months earlier. Still to be accomplished were all the Sulaco exterior shots, a handful of drop-ship scenes and a few specialized setups requiring computer-controlled repeatability. \"The shot that starts with Ripley in her sleep capsule, then match-dissolves to the curvature of the earth and pans over to the Gateway station, was one of the most complex we've ever attempted,\" said Bob Skotak. \"The basic idea was to combine several elements - the earth, the Gateway station, the starfields and some ships moving through the shot - all in-camera using a beam-splitter. The earth started out as a nine-foot painting by Pete Melrose that was then photographed, printed on color print stock and mounted on a starfield. By photographing it first, we were able to alter the size - which we reduced by about half - and also make whatever refinements we thought were necessary. In fact, I did quite a lot of retouching on itrearranging the clouds and changing the lighting a bit. The lefthand portion of the Gateway was another piece of artwork painted on glass - also by Pete Melrose - while the structures on the right-hand side were models reflected through a beamsplitter. Each element had its own separate starfield which we were able to join up without too much trouble since they were high-contrast and as long as we put stars in the right places all the bright spots would blend together. Finally we had the ships that moved through on tracks. Without the beam-splitter, it would have been virtually impossible to light all those elements and not have the lights themselves in the shot. It also saved"],["61","Aliens","CINEFEX 27 • 61"],["62","Aliens","62 - CINEFEX 27"],["63","Aliens","time, because we could see how everything fit together and we could adjust the various parts right then and there. We didn't have to wait for an optical or a double-exposure later on. By shifting the elements we could change the perspective or maybe get a ship to work better, and we had the advantage of actually seeing the whole thing through the camera.\" For the Skotaks and their assistant, Rashid Khares, the Gateway shot became an exercise in shooting precisely to time. \"The picture was locked at that point,\" Bob Skotak affirmed, \"so we knew exactly how long our shot had to be, right to the frame. Since it was to be done entirely in-camera, we had to lay out the elements in such a way that they were all adjustable. The artwork of the earth, the artwork of the station, the model of the station, the ships, the starfields - all were set up so that we could expand them out for a wider pan or compress them for a shorter pan. We tried varying combinations of distance and speed to find one that looked right without strobing. That's one of the reasons we did the shot at Arkadon. With their field recorder system we could record our final move and then repeat it as many times as was needed. We did three passes - a beauty pass for the earth and all the structures; a light pass for the little red warning lights and the other practicals on the miniature; then another pass for the countermatte of the starfield. It went quite well, although there were a few glitches. We started out with a backlit effect, but then Jim decided it would look better if the sun were more central in the shot so that light would rake across the structures. That was no problem for the miniature, but for the artwork - where the lighting effect was painted on - it meant redoing it almost from scratch. Time was short, so in one seventeen-hour period, I repainted over the whole thing and we shot it again. And Jim was right - it looked much better.\" Later, on the Sulaco, another nontraditional technique was employed to incorporate live-action elements into a full panoramic establishing shot of the miniature cargo hold. During principal photography, a locked-off Vistavision plate had been shot on the full-size cargo hold set as the colonial marines, fresh from their hypersleep, assemble around the drop-ship for a mission briefing. \"After our twelfth-scale cargo hold was constructed,\" said Bob Skotak, \"we photographed a series of stills of it, panning all the way around from the rear bay up to the front. It took five shots to get the full panorama. We then enlarged those photos and pasted them together, cheating the perspective so it wouldn't be evident that what we had was a segmented curve. The idea then was to use a beam-splitter to combine the still photographs with the live-action plate material, thereby revealing the entire cargo hold in one 90-degree pan. What was tricky about the shot was that we were going from still photograph to motion picture plate in the middle, and the point where they joined cut right through the drop-ship. In order to make it work, we had to match both halves very carefully and create a blend between the two - and it was the worst possible situation because we were blending over a straight line. For certain actions that the full-size characters were unable to achieve, quarterscale rod- and cable-operated puppets - designed and constructed at Doug Beswick Productions - were employed. / The completed queen armature awaiting foam skin and fiberglass head. One of the most difficult aspects of building the queen was trying to fit a multitude of cables and actuating mechanisms inside a character whose design allowed very little room for them. / Cosmetics supervisor Tony Gardner glues down a rubber cowl to the queen's lightweight fiberglass head piece. / Construction supervisor Jim Belohovek at work on the loader's roll cage. / Mechanical systems designer Phil Notaro installs a rotating light on top of the nearly-completed power-loader. CINEFEX 27 • 63"],["64","Aliens","The puppet figures were put through their paces on a precisely rendered quarterscale replica of the cargo hold set. For the miniature queen's first scene, a full complement of puppeteers support and direct her from manually operate her legs and five more on joysticks controlled functions. / Onset puppet supervisor Phil Notaro makes adjustments to the characters prior to a scene in which Ripley tries to maneuver the queen into the cargo hold's airlock. Like the queen, the power-loader required two below-stage operators to walk it and an additional five on joysticks was required - two to above, two below to to actuate her cableto orchestrate its other 64 • CINEFEX 27 One of the things we did was go in and touch up the photograph with chalk and white paint to match the quality of the whites in the plate. Also, by spot-lighting, we were able to boost the tonal values of the photograph. Plus, we had thirty-six C-stands set up with various color correction filters and fingers to darken certain areas and make the blacks purer.\" Having matched as closely as possible the look of the still composite to the live-action plate, the Skotaks were ready to proceed with the shot. 'We placed the photographic pasteup in front of the camera,\" Dennis Skotak explained, \"with the beam-splitter in between. Off to the side was a rear process screen onto which the plate would be projected, plus a few quarter-scale cargo lock elements to help fill in the edges where the plate ended. Using a field recorder, we then panned around the photos and into a dark area where the beam-splitter was reflecting the projected image off the screen.\" \"It wasn't a normal motion control shot,\" Bob Skotak commented, \"since there wasn't any need to repeat the move. By using the field recorder, though, we were able to hand-operate the pan, then fine-tune that pan in extended time. In one of our tests, the beginning of the pan was a little abrupt and mechanical. Even though it was hand-operated, it didn't look like something that was shot by a camera on a set. So we toyed with it, trying to get a natural ease up and ease down, and then scaled everything down to the slow speed that was needed for the exposure. It was a practical way of getting the shot, and we were able to do it with just three people as opposed to other methods that would have required a whole stage full.\" More traditional motion control techniques would be employed to produce establishing shots of the Sulaco cruising through space and orbiting over Acheron. \"The Sulaco was one of the first designs to come in and one of the last to be built,\" said Pat McClung. \"It was also one of the few designs relatively untouched by Jim. He really seemed to like that one. From what I understand, he told Syd Mead at the start that he wanted the feel of a U-boat - which is pretty much what he got. Before we could build it, though, we had to do some of our own design work. It seems Syd hadn't drawn the back end of it, so Bob Skotak and I had to come up with a look for the engines. Jim just told usto give him something that didn't look like a heavy thruster - he didn't want to see a big engine bell like the Saturn 5. So we came up with a series of plates and vents and so forth that I think looked pretty good.\" Though many times larger in true scale, the Sulaco model would end up approximately the same size as the principal dropships - roughly six feet in length. \"Originally,\" McClung continued, \"Jim wanted to make it about ten feet long, but Bob and I convinced him we could do it a lot smaller and still have all the detail he wanted. As with most of our other models, the basic body was built by Pete Astin. Almost all the surfaces were made with a foamed plastic board that's lightweight and comes in various thicknesses up to about half an inch. At that point there was no detail on it whatsoever. And certain areas were not true to the drawing. This can be a problem with Syd Mead's designs because ten different people will interpret his drawings in ten different ways. Having worked on three of his earlier films, I felt I could legitimately change certain areas where I thought Peter had gone a bit astray - so I did. From there, I essentially added all the detailing - bits and pieces of plastic pipe and stuff like that.\" Since the Sulaco was to be used in only a handful of shots, it was determined that a single mounting point would suffice. For like reasons, all the shots were designed in such a way that only the right side of the ship needed to be detailed. The left side - always facing away from the camera - was left exactly as delivered. \"One of the things I'd always wanted was to do a model with low-relief detail - something where most of the detail would be jammed into cracks and crevices and things. Luckily, that's what Jim had in mind too. So the Sulaco is very low-relief, especially in the front. And since it was a military ship, Jim didn't want to have lights on it, which I also thought was nice. One of the main things I had to do was break up the surfaces. An overall sheen can really give away the scale. So what I did was polish a lot of the panels and then go back with a dulling spray and basically panel off again with a dull coat over a gloss finish - just to give the illusion of panel breakup. Another thing I did was to take a razor blade and at a very steep angle just run it against some of the higher edges. What that does is flatten the paint so that if you look at it from certain directions you get nice little light kicks. All these things help in retaining the scale.\" Though fully equipped to do bluescreen photography, Brian Johnson opted to work with frontlight/backlight traveling mattes on the Sulaco. \"Primarily,\" said Johnson, \"we went with frontlight/backlight because it's simpler and faster. Even though it requires two model passes instead of just one, the actual compositing is much easier. With the black-and-white processor we have at Arkadon, we can make our own high-con mattes from the backlit pass and then put the whole scene together in bipack, right in the camera - which is what we did on most of the Aliens shots. As a result, we were able to get a finished composite in about three or four days as compared to a couple of weeks or more with bluescreen where you have to generate all the mattes and composite everything optically. We had about twenty-four shots to do, and by the time we started there were only a couple months left in the schedule. So our object was to get the shots done and get approval on them as quickly as possible.\" In all, there were only a half-dozen shots of the Sulaco. \"We put the model on an eight-axis model mover,\" Johnson continued, \"and came up with various track configurations based on the requirements of the storyboards. Photographically, most of them were fairly routine, although we did do a couple of interesting things. One was to cover the model with tiny strips of cello-tape. Jim wanted the ship to have a somewhat reflective surface, and with the tape we were able to pick up a few little glints of light as it passed through. For the planet backgrounds, we used a couple of different approaches. On the closeup shots,"],["65","Aliens","where the planet filled the frame, the clouds were actually a mixture of baking powder and microballoons sprinkled onto a sheet of glass. Then, below that, we had a painting of the planet surface that was rear-projected. For longer shots, where the curvature of the planet was visible, we used dome projections. The dome itself was about six feet in diameter, and on either side of it we set up slide projectors and masked them off with little pieces of cardboard to produce a kind of penumbral shadow. Then, to create a slight sense of rotation, we mounted the slide projectors on stepper motor rigs that actually panned the still images across the surface.\" The basic matting techniques employed on the Sulaco would also be used on the few remaining drop-ship shots. Though the ships themselves would be shot via motion control, the background plates would not. Those, in fact, were already in the can, having been photographed at Pinewood prior to the move to Arkadon. Thus, computer-controlled models were bipacked with manually rigged background plates to produce finished composites of the drop-ship encircling the atmosphere processing station early in the film and later passing through one of the giant heat radiators and into the maw. Even the cloud bank into which the drop-ship disappears shortly after its initial launch was produced on the Pinewood effects stages. \"We took a lot of fiberfill and teased it out over an area about twenty-five by thirty feet,\" said Johnson. \"Then we got a big crane and tracked in on it and punched down through the stuff and did about fifty passes on that until we got some that Jim liked. Then we did a motion control pass on the drop-ship and just popped it in, adding a bit of diffusion and low-contrast flashing at the end to make it look as though it actually disappeared into the clouds.\" Of necessity, one of the drop-ship shots had to be done via bluescreen. \"The unfolding drop-ship model was not designed with motion control in mind since the original plan had been to shoot it in real time with a process background. I decided later that motion control would be a better way to go, but by then it was too late to go in and have the model rebuilt to our specifications. So when it came time to do the drop-ship unfolding its wings and gun pods, we had to use radio-control servos and little gadgets like that to motorize everything - which worked fine, but it wasn't a repeatable system so we had to go with bluescreen.\" Most complex of the motion control shots was the initial launch of the drop-ship - a severe up-angle view of the tiny craft as it falls away from the Sulaco, plunges rapidly toward camera and then veers off and descends into the atmosphere of Acheron. \"At first we were going to try to do itall in-camera,\" said Johnson, \"but it was just too complicated, so we ended up generating our mattes and making color separations and putting the whole thing together in the optical printer. That way we had a lot more control over it. The Sulaco was one element, the starfield was another. Then there was a move onto the planet, which was one of our dome projections with the rotating images. We also did a pass on the sun to rim-light the drop-ship as it went by. The CINEFEX 27 • 65"],["66","Aliens","66 • CINEFEX 27 CLEAR"],["67","Aliens","drop-ship itself was the hardest part, and we ended up doing it in two different ways. First we put it on a big rotating modelmover and then tracked our camera up to the model and back again - using motion control dimmers to fade the lighting in and out from different directions, depending on where the ship was supposed to be in the shot. By rotating the model around as we moved in and out, we were able to create the impression that the ship was dropping toward the camera and then going off to the side. The move had a tew problems with it and Jim decided he wanted to see the drop-ship accelerate quicker anyway, so we redid it. The second time we redesigned the move so the camera tracked up to the model and then continued on past it rather than returning along the same route. That seemed to be a better approach all around. We hadn't done it that way at first because we were doing it frontlight/backlight and it seemed easier to get the white pass if we were going forward and then back - although, in actuality, that didn't prove to be the case. Another reason for the up and back approach on the first one, though, was that we had a second setup on the other end of the stage and could therefore use only half of our hundredfoot track length. When we did it again, we used almost the whole thing.\" A similar low-angle view of the Sulaco would be seen much later in the film after Ripley has vanquished the alien queen and ejected her out of the airlock and into space. For a scene of the beast tumbling toward camera, the quarter-scale queen that had been used in the egg chamber was attached to the Arkadon model-mover. \"In addition to the rotation,\" Johnson noted, \"the queen had cable-operated head movements, cable-operated arms and legs, plus a few exterior wires to jiggle the tail a bit - all ofwhich were nonrepeatable. So that was another instance where we had to use bluescreen. We also did bluescreen passes on some falling boxes and other bits of flotsam and jetsam whirling around. The Sulaco, however, we shot frontlight/backlight and composited in-camera with the starfield. Both were rotating just to give an added element of confusion and we did an extra pass on the airlock area, lit from behind, with some freon gas being ejected through it. Also we added a sparkle element that was just shot against black.\" Johnson delivered the last of his effects shots in early May. Six weeks later the film was finished. Four weeks after that it opened to wildly enthusiastic critical and audience response, setting a boxoffice pace that would promptly propel it into the top echelon of the year's most popular films. Having cut their cinematic teeth on Roger Corman productions, it is not surprising that Gale Hurd and Jim Cameron share what amounts to an obsession for planting every dollar allocated to them squarely on the screen. By contemporary standards, The Terminator's $6.5 million pricetag relegated it to the ranks of low-budget independent filmmaking, yet it looked every bit as glossy as a major studio-backed production commanding twice the resources. With Aliens, their first foray into big-time moviemaking, they have produced - for $18 million - a motion picture that by any standards one cares to apply fares more than well when gauged against other mainstream science fiction films costing $25 million or more. \"The approach that we have to filmmaking - even with a budget as expanded as this one - is still'no frills,\" said Hurd. \"Instead of doing the typical things you would expect on a 'big-budget' film, we went the opposite direction and employed people we had worked with back at New World Pictures - people like Robert and Dennis Skotak. Yet we also brought in Brian Johnson, who worked on Alien and The Empire Strikes Back, to work with them. Then, for the creature effects, we went right to Stan Winston who had worked with us on The Terminator. So there was a lot of continuity, which really helped. I think with an effects film you can never really anticipate all the problem areas because it's totally conceptual to begin with and then you have to get down to the nuts and bolts of how you're going to shoot it. Luckily, with Jim's background in effects, he was able to troubleshoot quite a lot of that in advance. He could talk on a technical level with the people who were actually going to have to create the effects and make them work, and he was able to solve a lot of problems that way before any money had to be spent.\" The film's low-tech approach to visual effects - more akin to The Terminator or the original Alien than to other more recent science fiction extravaganzas such as 2010 - was crucial both to the cost-cutting effort and to Cameron's desire to maintain direct control over as much of the film as possible. \"It's not that we didn't have the money,\" Cameron commented, \"it's almost that we didn't want the money. I know nobody will believe that, but it's true. People tend to use the budget of a film as a yardstick for how good the movie is, but I think a lot of recent productions have really killed the viability of science fiction films in general because the people making them seem to believe in throwing enormous amounts of money at them just to get the job done. And it doesn't work that way. For one thing, it's diminishing returns beyond a certain point - the extra money just allows you to polish. All you're really doing is putting a slightly higher buff on something you already had. And sometimes the difference between a shot that costs twice as much and one that didn't is pretty negligible. Aliens is a blend of upscale and down-scale techniques - whatever a specific shot required. I guess it's what you'd have to calla 'no pride' approach. I love to have cable-actuated things that have been worked out and tested on video and take eight people to operate; but if the shot can be achieved by grabbing what is the equivalent of a rubber chicken and throwing it against the wall, that's fine - I'll do it that way, too. The very first thing you have to learn as a director is that you're never going to get it perfect. The place to make it perfect is in the cutting room or on the dubbing stage where you bring all the other tricks out of the hat. If the characters are good and the story works, the audience will go with it. If the story doesn't work, the best special effects in the world aren't going to keep them in their seats.\" Ripley's attempt to force the queen into the airlock and expel her into space backfires when the alien monarch disables the powerloader and both tumble into the lock. On a slanted set, Sigoumey Weaver tries to free herself from the queen - Stan Winston's fullsize creation, again controlled solely by internal operators and external wires. / The actual fall into the airlock and the queen's subsequent expulsion from it were achieved in miniature with the quarterscale airlock being removed from the cargo hold set and bolted to the stage ceiling. A floppy version of the creature was then released. / For a subsequent up-angle of the queen tumbling away from the Sulaco, a cablecontrolled puppet was photographed in front of a bluescreen at Arkadon, then composited with the miniature ship and starfield. CINEFEX 27 • 67"],["68","Closing pages",""]]});
